{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":1787429,"items":[{"DOI":"10.4018\/979-8-3373-1419-8.ch001","title":["Advancements in Multi-Agent Large Language Model Systems for Next-Generation AI"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9978-0187","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Rajeshwari","family":"Sissodia","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Galgotias University, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-7502-3222","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Vinay","family":"Dwivedi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Galgotias University, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"TaraChand","family":"Verma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Galgotias University, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4018\/979-8-3373-1419-8.ch001","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,25]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The integration of Multi-Agent Systems (MAS) with Large Language Models (LLMs) represents a significant advancement in the field of artificial intelligence, enabling the development of intelligent, autonomous systems capable of solving complex tasks and enhancing decision-making. MAS, composed of multiple interacting agents with autonomous decision-making abilities, and LLMs, which leverage vast amounts of textual data to understand and generate human-like language, can work synergistically to create more robust AI systems. This chapter explores the fundamentals of MAS and LLMs, their individual and combined strengths, and their potential to address complex challenges in various fields, such as robotics, healthcare, and business. We discuss key features of both MAS and LLMs, including agent collaboration, reinforcement learning, and contextual understanding in language models. The chapter also examines the integration of these two domains, highlighting their potential for collaborative problem-solving, decision support, and knowledge extraction.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Advances in Computational Intelligence and Robotics","Advancements in Multi-Agent Large Language Model Systems for Next-Generation AI"]},{"DOI":"10.22541\/au.177403077.70686468\/v1","title":["Emergent Cooperative Dynamics and Causal Treatment Effects in Large Language Model Multi-Agent Ecosystems"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-8451-8373","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Vikas","family":"Ramachandra","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.22541\/au.177403077.70686468\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,20]]}},{"DOI":"10.4018\/979-8-3373-1419-8.ch002","title":["Advancements in Multi-Agent Large Language Model Systems for Next-Generation AI"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0334-3637","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Abinaya","family":"Gopalakrishnan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"SRM Institute of Science and Technology, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4712-8699","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"G.","family":"Ramya","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Networking and Communications, SRM Institute of Science and Technology, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3504-1884","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"T.","family":"Preethiya","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Networking and Communications, SRM Institute of Science and Technology, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2281-1923","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"R.","family":"Nithya Paranthaman","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"SRM Institute of Science and Technology, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"S.","family":"Ashwini","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"SRM Institute of Science and Technology, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"R.","family":"Dhwarithaa","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"SRM Institute of Science and Technology, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4018\/979-8-3373-1419-8.ch002","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,25]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Large Language Models (LLMs) have enabled AI research. Their easier design of new ways to handle challenges across a wide range of applications has increased this discipline's influence. Multi-agent LLM systems in diagnostics and healthcare can revolutionize clinical decision-making, precision medicine, and patient care. This chapter examines multi-agent LLMs' medical concepts, designs, and applications.Multi-agent systems can scale, modularize, and specialize and integrate several medical specializations and contextual knowledge. This chapters covers the technical implementation of these systems, including advanced Large Language Models, quality control measures, guardrails, self-reflection, integration with EHRs, and explainable AI for decision transparency. We discuss possible benefits with future directions, like integrating IoT devices and creating advanced natural language interfaces.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Advances in Computational Intelligence and Robotics","Advancements in Multi-Agent Large Language Model Systems for Next-Generation AI"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6645998","title":["A Multi-Agent Large Language Model Framework for NCAA Tournament Simulation&amp;nbsp;"],"author":[{"given":"Abhyut","family":"Tangri","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6645998","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>We present a full empirical evaluation of a multi-agent large language model (LLM) framework applied to the 2026 NCAA Division I Men's Basketball Tournament. Three specialized agentsSimulationAgent, AnalysisAgent, and OrchestratorAgentpowered by Meta's Llama 3.3 70B (Groq API, temperature = 0.7) simulated the complete 63-game bracket four independent times, producing 252 structured game outcomes. Michigan (#1 Midwest) defeated UConn (#2 East) 6963 in the actual championship; Arizona (#1 West) and Illinois (#3 South) completed the actual Final Four. The simulation correctly predicted Michigan as champion in one of four runs (25%). UConn appeared in all four simulated Final Fours (100%). However, Arizona and Illinois appeared in zero simulationsinstead, Houston and Purdue (neither an actual Final Four team) appeared in all four. Three of four runs produced identical Final Fours, revealing severe convergence at temperature = 0.7. The #9\/#8 upset rate was 94% versus a historical 50%; seeds 1315 produced zero upsets combined versus historical expectations of 1420% per matchup. Mean winning margin was 11.7 points across 252 games; zero overtime games were simulated. All gures are veried against raw simulation JSON les.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5584538","title":["Forecasting\u00a0carbon market with a multi-agent system of large language model"],"author":[{"given":"Bangzhu","family":"Zhu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jiangtao","family":"Zhong","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5584538","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]}},{"DOI":"10.4018\/979-8-3373-1419-8.ch008","title":["Security and Privacy Challenges in Multi-Agent Language Model Ecosystems"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3601-102X","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Pawan Kumar","family":"Goel","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Raj Kumar Goel Institute of Technology, Ghaziabad, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4018\/979-8-3373-1419-8.ch008","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,25]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The integration of multi-agent systems with language models is a significant area in artificial intelligence, enabling collaborative problem-solving and decision-making. However, this integration also presents security and privacy challenges, such as data leakage, adversarial attacks, and unauthorized access. Existing literature highlights these issues, such as the lack of robust frameworks for data integrity, the difficulty of balancing transparency with privacy, and the absence of mechanisms to detect and mitigate malicious agent behavior. To address these, a novel decentralized privacy-preserving framework is proposed, leveraging homomorphic encryption and differential privacy. Experimental results show that this framework reduces data leakage by 40% and improves adversarial attack resilience by 35% compared to existing methods, while maintaining high computational efficiency. This approach enhances security and privacy in multi-agent systems, setting a new benchmark for future research.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Advances in Computational Intelligence and Robotics","Advancements in Multi-Agent Large Language Model Systems for Next-Generation AI"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5193257","title":["Communication Structure Adaptive Control and Collaborative Optimization for Multi-Agent Systems Based on Deepseek Large Language Model"],"author":[{"given":"Yi","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5193257","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]}},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6186858","title":["Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning for Cooperative Large Language Model Collaboration"],"author":[{"given":"Thomas J.","family":"Bennett","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Samuel K.","family":"O\u2019Neill","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Laura M.","family":"Harding","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6186858","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>This study investigates cooperative LLM collaboration through a multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) framework, where agents are jointly optimized using a shared group reward rather than independent objectives. A multi-agent GRPO (MAGRPO) algorithm is proposed to stabilize learning under decentralized execution. Evaluation is performed on 4 collaborative benchmarks covering reasoning, writing, and code synthesis, totaling 5,200 training episodes. Results show that MAGRPO improves average task reward by 27.6%, reduces inter-agent contradiction rates by 31.2%, and accelerates convergence by 1.8\u00d7 compared to independent RL fine-tuning.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.64898\/2026.02.22.26346818","title":["Agent Role Structure and Operating Characteristics in Large Language Model Clinical Classification: A Comparative Study of Specialist and Deliberative Multi-Agent Protocols"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-7858-1575","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Callum","family":"Anderson","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Engineering, University of Ottawa, 800 King Edward Avenue, Ottawa, Ontario K1N 6N5, Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.64898\/2026.02.22.26346818","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,24]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                <jats:p>\n                  Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly considered in clinical decision support, yet the architectural effects of role decomposition within multi-agent systems remain poorly isolated. Prior comparisons of single-agent and multi-agent prompting often confound workflow structure with changes in model configuration, training, or decoding. We present a controlled study evaluating whether specialist and generalist multi-agent protocols produce different operating characteristics under fixed inference conditions. Two deterministic protocols, Generic Deliberative (GD) and Feature-Specialist (FS), are evaluated under identical base weights, decoding settings, computational budget, and adjudication logic. The primary comparison isolates role structure and information routing, while feature-assignment and model-scale ablations evaluate boundary conditions. On the UCI Cleveland Heart Disease and Pima Indians Diabetes benchmarks, altering role structure produces substantial operating-characteristic shifts. Under the primary configuration (\n                  <jats:monospace>llama3.1:8b<\/jats:monospace>\n                  ), Cleveland FS achieves higher accuracy (0.72 vs. 0.65) and macro-F1 (0.71 vs. 0.65), with a specificity-favoring operating point, whereas Pima GD achieves higher accuracy (0.68 vs. 0.51) and macro-F1 (0.64 vs. 0.49), while FS induces pronounced class asymmetry. Feature-pair ablations show that specialist feature assignment is a design lever, with FS accuracy ranging from 0.41 to 0.72. A model-scale ablation (\n                  <jats:monospace>qwen2.5:14b<\/jats:monospace>\n                  ) shows that protocol-level effects interact with base-model capacity. Overall, internal role decomposition should not be treated as safety-neutral: it can act as a feature-conditioned architectural bias that changes evidence aggregation and shifts class-wise error profiles, indicating that prompt structure and architectural configuration should be explicitly tuned, validated, and reported in safety-sensitive LLM-based clinical decision systems.\n                <\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1038\/s41598-025-96458-w","title":["MMAgentRec, a personalized multi-modal recommendation agent with large language model"],"author":[{"given":"Xiaochen","family":"Xiao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1038\/s41598-025-96458-w","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,4,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,4,8]]},"container-title":["Scientific Reports"]},{"DOI":"10.20944\/preprints202604.0757.v1","title":["Intelligent Tunnel Fire Detection Technology Based on the Large Language Model Multi-Agent Collaboration"],"author":[{"given":"Ding","family":"Zeng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ao","family":"Gao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zhisheng","family":"Xu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.20944\/preprints202604.0757.v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,10]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>To address the issues of manual operation dependency and low efficiency in tunnel fire research combining computational fluid dynamics (CFD)with deep learning, this paper proposes a multi-agent collaborative framework based on large language models to automate the entire process of inverting fire source characteristics. The framework decomposes the traditional workflow into four specialized agents, namely physical modeling, data governance, model training, and evaluation analysis, which collaboratively execute end-to-end tasks from CFD scenario generation to model deployment. The results demonstrate that the CNN-LSTM model performs optimally. Under a 6 second observation window and 10 meter sensor spacing, the average R\u00b2 reaches 0.942, representing a 2% improvement over the baseline LSTM model, while the RMSE is reduced by 28.8%. Under sparse deployment with 30 meter spacing, the average R\u00b2 remains as high as 0.917, validating the effectiveness of integrating\u00a0spatial feature extraction with temporal modeling. This work provides an efficient technological pathway for intelligent tunnel fire identification and advances the research paradigm from manual optimization to multi-agent system optimization.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5193679","title":["A Multi-Agent Large Language Model (Llm) Framework for Code-Complying Design Automation of Concrete Structures"],"author":[{"given":"Jinxin","family":"Chen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yi","family":"Bao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5193679","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]}},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7009758","title":["No Assembly Bonus: A Pre-registered, Seven-regime Disconfirmation of Super-Additivity in Multi-agent Large-Language-Model Decision Boards"],"author":[{"given":"Andrew","family":"Guitarte","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7009758","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>A widely shared assumption in artificial intelligence holds that a well-organized group of agents decides better than its best individual member - the assembly bonus. We tested that assumption in its sharp machine form. Before any data were collected, we pre-registered the hypothesis that a board of multiple artificial-intelligence agents, deliberating together, reaches decisions of higher quality than its single best, well-calibrated member acting alone (H1), together with a rescue hypothesis that deliberate de-correlation across model families would produce the bonus that homogeneous boards lack (H1a). We evaluated both across seven regimes - a baseline board, single-family and cross-family boards, a board with an added devil's advocate, a quorum-gated board, varied domains and difficulty, and stacked combinations - scoring each configuration against an external expert verdict on the same accept-or-reject decisions. Across all seven regimes, H1 and H1a were disconfirmed. The board tied or lost to its best single calibrated member, and in several regimes it decided measurably worse. A genuine three-model-family panel achieved real error de-correlation (mean inter-agent error correlation 0.85 versus 1.00 for clones, replicated across three seeds) yet produced no gain; in contested cases the agents were even anti-correlated and the board remained sub-additive. Four rescues - cross-family diversity, a devil's advocate, a quorum gate, and stricter aggregation - each failed on its own terms. The mechanism is miscalibration under aggregation: pooling many individually well-calibrated agents accumulates their concerns and drifts the collective toward over-rejection, hardest on the borderline cases where assurance matters most. One result survived the wreckage: forcing every agent to ground each claim in quoted evidence, or stay silent, cut fabricated objections by roughly half. The board was a poor oracle and an excellent assurance instrument - it could not vote its way to a better verdict, but it tracked every concern, applied every rule identically, and produced a complete, inspectable record. We report the corrected, falsifiable claim and designate a confirmatory study on real, independently-labeled enterprise cases.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.20944\/preprints202605.0900.v1","title":["A Large Language Model-Enabled Multi-Agent Collaboration Method for Complex Task Solving"],"author":[{"given":"Shuyuan","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yihui","family":"Feng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Xiaotian","family":"Fang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.20944\/preprints202605.0900.v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,13]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Aiming at the core limitations of single large language models in complex task solving, including coarse task decomposition, cumulative long-chain reasoning errors, and the lack of explicit cross-agent collaboration, this paper proposes a large language model-driven multi-agent collaborative method. A hierarchical and role-based agent architecture is designed to separate task decomposition, specialized reasoning, result verification, and decision fusion, thereby enabling modular task solving and closed-loop orchestration over the full execution process. In addition, an efficient semantic communication mechanism is introduced to transmit compressed reasoning states across agents without breaking intermediate logical dependencies. A dynamic feedback iteration module is further employed to adjust routing strategy, collaboration intensity, and reasoning paths in real time according to subtask progress and verification outcomes. Comparative experiments on mathematical reasoning, multi-step planning, and complex information integration show that, relative to a single large language model, the proposed method improves the average completion rate by 21.3%, reduces the long-chain reasoning error rate by 18.7%, and reaches 92.6% cross-agent decision consistency. These results demonstrate that structured collaboration substantially improves robustness and accuracy for complex task solving and provides a practical technical path for diverse intelligent systems.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6691280","title":["Comparative Evaluation of Large Language Model Augmentation Strategies: RAG, Knowledge Graph, Multi-Step Agent, Fine-Tuning, and Hybrid Approaches"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-6547-1650","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Hari Prasad","family":"Sampatirao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6691280","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The rapid proliferation of large language models (LLMs) has generated substantial interest in techniques for grounding model responses in domain-specific knowledge. This paper presents a systematic empirical comparison of five LLM augmentation strategies applied to document-grounded question answering over a corpus of academic research documents: (1) Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) with FAISS vector search and bibliography filtering, (2) Knowledge Graph (KG) augmentation using NetworkX-based co-occurrence graphs, (3) a Multi-Step Reasoning Agent employing iterative plan-searchreason-synthesise cycles, (4) parameter-efficient fine-tuning of TinyLlama-1.1B via Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA), and (5) a Hybrid approach combining all three retrieval strategies to feed the fine-tuned model. Using TinyLlama\/TinyLlama-1.1B-Chat-v1.0 as the base model across all conditions, we evaluate each approach on five domain-relevant research questions using keyword-overlap relevance scoring and response-time measurement. The Multi-Step Agent achieved the highest average relevance score (0.88), followed by the Hybrid approach (0.67) and Fine-tuning (0.46), while vanilla RAG performed the worst on direct relevance despite lower latency. Our analysis identifies trade-offs between answer quality, computational cost, and deployment complexity, yielding practical guidance for researchers and practitioners selecting LLM augmentation strategies for knowledge-intensive tasks.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5482172","title":["Large language model multi agent architecture for automatic well\u2011logging analysis featuring embedded physics priors plus multimodal fusion of data"],"author":[{"given":"Yen","family":"Ceao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5482172","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]}},{"DOI":"10.20944\/preprints202606.1385.v1","title":["PetroAgents: A Multi-Agent, Multi-Modal Large-Language-Model Framework for Integrated Upstream Petroleum Asset Reasoning, Benchmarked on the Equinor Volve Open Dataset"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5473-7345","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Rong","family":"Lu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.20944\/preprints202606.1385.v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,18]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>We present PetroAgents, a multi-agent, multi-modal large-language-model framework for petroleum-engineering reasoning on the Equinor Volve open dataset. The target architecture mirrors an integrated asset team: discipline-specific evidence surfaces, cross-examination, a Council Synthesiser, distributional well-action proposals, and risk review. Read as an agent-design pattern map, the architecture is organised around the seven cognitive functions of a language agent, with the per-discipline evidence lock as its governance layer; four functions are implemented and evaluated in this submission and three are specified as design. The current quantitative evidence is narrower by design. We specify Volve Bench, a six-task Volve benchmark suite spanning DRILL-NPT root-cause attribution, formation top picking, six-month production forecasting, stuck-pipe early warning, multi-modal Discovery-Report QA, and per-wellbore lifecycle forensic analysis over 26 wellbores, 1,759 daily drilling reports, 56 million WITSML rows, 602 LAS files, 5.7 million horizon points, and a decade of production. This submission reports two landed evidence slices: a three-seed DRILL-NPT study on stratified samples drawn from a 1,750-example pool, and a 12-question DISCOVERY-QA smoke test on three rendered pages of the 194-page Hugin Discovery Report. Every reported LLM call goes through a local OpenAI-compatible gateway using locally-hosted open weights (GPT-OSS-120B, Qwen3.6-35B, Gemma-4-31B, MiniMax-M2.7, Qwen3-VL-235B-FP8); no paid frontier API is invoked. On DRILL-NPT, the four-family vote attains macro-F1 0.464 \u00b1 0.012 on the broad all-wellbore sample and 0.442 \u00b1 0.019 on the WITSML-applicable subset. The Drilling+HSE+Council path lifts WITSML-applicable macro-F1 by +0.048 over the single-LLM baseline, but the paired test is not significant at three seeds (p = 0.22), so we report it as directional evidence rather than a settled win. A same-subset evidence-redaction ablation shows that exposing state_detail and proprietary_code lifts B1 from 0.355 to 0.431 macro-F1, quantifying how much of DRILL-NPT is label-code leakage rather than prose reasoning. On DISCOVERY-QA, Qwen3-VL reading rendered page images reaches a 0.958 keyword-hit score versus 0.792 for GPT-OSS-120B reading pdftotext, a bounded +16.7 percentage-point lift concentrated on figure annotations and OCR-damaged numerics.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.64898\/2026.04.17.26351127","title":["Uncertainty-Gated Glaucoma Screening: Combining Semi-Supervised Classification with Multi-Agent Large Language Model Deliberation"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-5133-9931","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sai Varun","family":"Garimella Narasimha","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Northeastern University, Boston, Massachusetts, USA"},{"name":"Humanitarians AI, Boston, Massachusetts, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Nicholas","family":"Brown","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Northeastern University, Boston, Massachusetts, USA"},{"name":"Humanitarians AI, Boston, Massachusetts, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1605-0734","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Srinivas","family":"Sridhar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Northeastern University, Boston, Massachusetts, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.64898\/2026.04.17.26351127","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,20]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                <jats:p>\n                  Automated glaucoma screening from optical coherence tomography (OCT) faces two persistent challenges: scarcity of expert-labeled data and unreliable model predictions on diagnostically ambiguous cases. We present a two-tier diagnostic pipeline that addresses both. In the first tier, an EfficientNetV2-S classifier trained under a semi-supervised pseudo supervisor framework achieves 0.84 AUC on 150 held-out test patients from the Harvard Glaucoma Detection and Progression dataset, using only 350 labeled training samples out of 700. In the second tier, 124 flagged cases are routed to a multi-agent system built on MedGemma 4B, where three specialist agents deliberate over three rounds before rendering a final diagnosis. On these flagged cases, the agent system achieves 100% sensitivity\u2014detecting all 55 glaucoma cases with zero missed diagnoses\u2014and 89.5% overall accuracy (111\/124), compared to the classifier\u2019s 73.4% (91\/124). Uncertainty analysis confirms that the classifier\u2019s output probability reliably separates confident predictions (96.3% accuracy,\n                  <jats:italic>n<\/jats:italic>\n                  = 27) from uncertain ones (74.0%,\n                  <jats:italic>n<\/jats:italic>\n                  = 123), producing a 22-percentage-point gap that serves as a triage signal. The agents fix 32 cases the classifier misclassifies while introducing 12 new errors, yielding a net improvement of 20 cases. These results are from a single training run without variance estimates and should be interpreted as preliminary evidence that uncertainty-gated routing to vision-language model agents can meaningfully improve diagnostic accuracy on the cases where automated classifiers are least reliable.\n                <\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.54254\/2755-2721\/109\/20241330","title":["Large Language Model Applied in Multi-agent System\u2014A Survey"],"author":[{"given":"Kaiwen","family":"Dong","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Lancaster University Business School"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.54254\/2755-2721\/109\/20241330","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,26]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The application of large language models (LLMs) in single-agent systems within complex environments has proven successful, prompting a growing interest in their use within multi-agent systems (MAS). Despite the impressive capabilities of LLMs, it remains unclear how they can be optimally integrated and utilized to empower agents in MAS. Understanding how to effectively leverage the advantages of LLMs to enhance agent performance is crucial. This survey provides a comprehensive overview of the application of LLMs in MAS, focusing on their impact on agent cooperation, reasoning, and adaptive abilities. Finally, we discuss future directions and open questions in this evolving field.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Applied and Computational Engineering"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-981-96-5871-8_6","title":["Large Language Model for Embodied Multi-Agent System"],"author":[{"given":"Huaping","family":"Liu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Xinzhu","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Kangyao","family":"Huang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Di","family":"Guo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-981-96-5871-8_6","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,4,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Machine Learning: Foundations, Methodologies, and Applications","Embodied Multi-Agent Systems"]},{"DOI":"10.52202\/085713-3990","title":["LLM-DAMVC: A Large Language Model Assisted Dynamic Agent for Multi-View Clustering"],"author":[{"given":"Qianqian","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"ByteDance Inc."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Qianqian","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Xidian University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.52202\/085713-3990","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 38"]},{"DOI":"10.26434\/chemrxiv.15001153\/v1","title":["STALAgent: A Multi-Agent System Based on Large Language Model (LLM) for Steel and Alloy Design"],"author":[{"given":"Jiayi","family":"Qiu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Software","place":["Nanjing, China"]},{"name":"Nanjing University of Information Science & Technology","place":["Nanjing, China"]},{"name":"Department of Materials Physics, School of Chemistry and Materials Science","place":["Nanjing, China"]},{"name":"Nanjing University of Information Science & Technology","place":["Nanjing, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Youle","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Software","place":["Nanjing, China"]},{"name":"Nanjing University of Information Science & Technology","place":["Nanjing, China"]},{"name":"Department of Materials Physics, School of Chemistry and Materials Science","place":["Nanjing, China"]},{"name":"Nanjing University of Information Science & Technology","place":["Nanjing, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Fan","family":"Jiang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Song","family":"Wei","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lei","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute of Optics and Precision Mechanics","place":["Xi'an, Xi'an 710119, China"]},{"name":"Xi\u2019an Institute of Optics and Precision Mechanics of the Chinese Academy of Sciences (CAS)","place":["Xi'an, Xi'an 710119, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.26434\/chemrxiv.15001153\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,23]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The design of steel and alloy materials is of critical importance across a wide range of industrial applications; however, effective intelligent agent-based assistants for this domain remain limited. To address this gap, we introduce STALAgent, a large language model (LLM)-based multi-agent system specifically tailored for intelligent and automated design of steel and alloy materials. STALAgent is centered on an LLM brain with several key agents (e.g., task assignment, semantic search, inverse design and heat treatment simulation) that collectively form a closed-loop workflow from user query to material recommendation. This system leverages a CrewAI-based orchestrator to intelligently assign tasks and coordinate a suite of specialized agents, including tools for knowledge retrieval using retrieval augmented generation (RAG), inverse materials design using variational encoder (VAE) and thermodynamic calculations using Pycalphad. Through case studies involving inverse alloy design tasks and knowledge-based steel design queries, we showcase the capacity of the LLM agent to offer effective and dependable guidance for steel and alloy material design. STALAgent is practical and scalable, serving as a supplementary tool for materials researchers, and holding promise for extension to other materials science domains requiring scientific discovery and domain knowledge-intensive tasks.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2196\/preprints.75932","title":["A Multi-agent Large Language Model Framework for Medical Text Summarization and Evaluation: Development and Evaluation Study (Preprint)"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-0605-0842","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yuhao","family":"Chen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2017-1822","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Bo","family":"Wen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Farhana","family":"Zulkernine","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2196\/preprints.75932","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,4,15]]},"abstract":"<sec>\n                    <title>BACKGROUND<\/title>\n                        <p>Although Large Language Models (LLMs) show great promises in processing medical text, they are prone to generating incorrect information, commonly referred to as hallucinations. These inaccuracies present a significant risk for clinical applications where precision is critical. Additionally, relying on human experts to review LLM-generated content to ensure accuracy is costly and time-consuming, which sets a barrier against large-scale deployment of LLMs in healthcare settings.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                                <sec>\n                    <title>OBJECTIVE<\/title>\n                        <p>The primary objective of this study is to develop an automatic Artificial Intelligence (AI) system capable of extracting structured information from unstructured medical data and employing advanced reasoning techniques to support reliable clinical decision making. A key aspect of this objective is ensuring that the system incorporates self-verification mechanisms, enabling it to assess the accuracy and reliability of its own outputs. By integrating such mechanisms, we aim to enhance the system\u2019s robustness, reduce reliance on human intervention, and improve the overall trustworthiness of AI-driven medical summarization and evaluation.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                                <sec>\n                    <title>METHODS<\/title>\n                        <p>The proposed framework comprises two layers: a summarization layer and an evaluation layer. The summarization layer employs Llama2-70B and Mistral-7B models to generate concise summaries from unstructured medical data, focusing on tasks such as consumer health question summarization, biomedical answer summarization, and dialog summarization. The evaluation layer uses GPT-4-turbo as a judge, leveraging pairwise comparison strategies and different prompt strategies to evaluate summaries across four dimensions: coherence, consistency, fluency, and relevance. To validate the framework, we compare the judgments generated by the LLMs in the evaluation layer with those provided by medical experts, offering valuable insights into the alignment and reliability of AI-driven evaluations within the medical domain. We also explore a way to handle disagreement among human experts and discuss our methodology in addressing diversity in human perspectives.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                                <sec>\n                    <title>RESULTS<\/title>\n                        <p>The study found variability in expert consensus, with average Agreement Rates (ARs) of 19.2% among all experts and 51.6% among groups of three experts. GPT-4 demonstrated alignment with expert judgments, achieving an average AR of 78.44% with at least one expert and comparable performance in cross-validation tests. The enhanced guidance in prompt design improved GPT-4\u2019s alignment with expert evaluations, highlighting the importance of effective prompt engineering in auto-evaluation of summarization tasks.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                                <sec>\n                    <title>CONCLUSIONS<\/title>\n                        <p>This study highlights the potential of LLMs as reliable tools for medical summarization and evaluation, reducing the dependency on human experts. The proposed framework demonstrates scalability and adaptability for clinical applications while addressing key challenges like hallucination and position bias.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                                <sec>\n                    <title>INTERNATIONAL REGISTERED REPORT<\/title>\n                        <p>RR2-https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/ICDH62654.2024.00030<\/p>\n                <\/sec>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6625578","title":["A Hybrid Early-Warning System for Inflation in an Emerging Market: Combining Econometric Models, an Agent-Based Decomposition with Heterogeneous Expectations, a Large Language Model, and a Multi-Output Agent Architecture"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-8200-7942","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Esteban","family":"Labastidas","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6625578","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>We develop and evaluate a hybrid early-warning system for year-over-year (YoY) inflation in Colombia that combines four econometric models (ARIMA, LASSO, ElasticNet, and a weighted ensemble), a reduced-form VAR, an agent-based model with heterogeneous expectations and tradable\/non-tradable pass-through (ABM v2), a large language model (LLM) forecaster, and a multi-output agent architecture, integrated through Dynamic Model Averaging (DMA). We evaluate the system on a rolling out-of-sample backtest from February 2010 to March 2026 (n \u2248 194 months) spanning the 2021-2023 inflation surge and its ongoing disinflation. Five contributions emerge. First, an identity-based monthly-to-YoY decomposition applied uniformly across reduced-form models reduces MAE by 15-20% relative to direct YoY forecasting without adding variables. Second, regime-conditional analysis shows that MAE is 2.0-3.0 times larger in surge regimes than in stable regimes across all models. Third, an ABM with regime-dependent heterogeneous expectations reduces full-sample MAE from 0.337 pp (v1) to 0.268 pp (v2,-20.4%) and surge-regime MAE from 0.585 pp to 0.404 pp (-31.0%), with Diebold-Mariano statistic 6.21 (p &amp;lt; 0.001). The accompanying four-channel shock decomposition attributes the 2021-2023 surge primarily to amplification of wage (1.96\u00d7) and expectations (1.82\u00d7) channels rather than direct import pass-through (1.36\u00d7). Fourth, a structured-reasoning LLM forecaster is validated via live-API replication and a 60-call fake-date test; the non-monotonic MAE pattern is shown to reflect target-volatility confounding rather than look-ahead bias. Fifth, a multi-output agent architecture predicts BanRep monetary policy decisions with 77.3% direction accuracy (17\/22) and 63.6% exact-magnitude match (14\/22) on 2022-2026 meetings, with all prediction errors concentrated at cycle inflection points. The full DMA ensemble achieves MAE 0.26 pp and 94.8% coverage at horizon h = 1.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2514\/6.2026-0444","title":["Context-Based and Event-Driven Multi-Agent Architectures for Large Language Model Assistants in Engineering Environments"],"author":[{"given":"Stanislaus","family":"Reitenbach","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Deutsches Zentrum fur Luft- und Raumfahrt DLR"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Martin","family":"Siggel","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Deutsches Zentrum fur Luft- und Raumfahrt DLR"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Martin","family":"Bolemant","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Deutsches Zentrum fur Luft- und Raumfahrt DLR"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2514\/6.2026-0444","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,12]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,8]]},"container-title":["AIAA SCITECH 2026 Forum"]},{"DOI":"10.21838\/uhpc.25653","title":["Automated Structural Optimization of Ultra-high-performance Concrete Using a Large Language Model-powered Multi-agent Framework"],"author":[{"given":"Yi","family":"Bao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Civil, Environmental and Ocean Engineering, Stevens Institute of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jinxin","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Civil, Environmental and Ocean Engineering, Stevens Institute of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21838\/uhpc.25653","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,14]]},"container-title":["Fourth International Interactive Symposium on UHPC"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/iucc65928.2024.00072","title":["Verify-Agent: Large Language Model Multi-Agent for Intelligent Verification"],"author":[{"given":"Weiyan","family":"Chu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"China Unicom Research Institute,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sitan","family":"Yin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beijing University of Posts and Telecommunications,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lei","family":"Huang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"China Unicom Research Institute,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ling","family":"Lin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"China Unicom Research Institute,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiaodong","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"China Unicom Research Institute,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zhi","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"China Unicom Research Institute,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Hongwu","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"China Unicom Research Institute,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/iucc65928.2024.00072","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,12,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,12,20]]},"container-title":["2024 International Conference on Ubiquitous Computing and Communications (IUCC)"]},{"DOI":"10.3390\/fire9060233","title":["Intelligent Tunnel Fire Source Characteristic Inversion Technology Based on Large Language Model Multi-Agent Collaboration"],"author":[{"given":"Ding","family":"Zeng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute of Disaster Prevention Science and Safety Technology, Central South University, Changsha 410075, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ao","family":"Gao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Shenyang Fire Research Institute of MEM, National Engineering Research Center for Fire and Emergency Rescue, Shenyang 110034, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zhisheng","family":"Xu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute of Disaster Prevention Science and Safety Technology, Central South University, Changsha 410075, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/fire9060233","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,1]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The integration of computational fluid dynamics (CFD) with deep learning in tunnel fire research is currently constrained by excessive reliance on manual operations and low overall efficiency. To address these limitations, this study presents a multi-agent collaborative framework driven by large language models, which enables full automation of the fire source characteristic inversion process. This framework reorganizes the conventional research pipeline into four dedicated, specialized agents: physical modeling, data governance, model training, and evaluation analysis. As a typical automated verification task, five deep learning models are systematically benchmarked under 45 experimental configurations to implement multi-task continuous regression inversion, which fully demonstrates the framework\u2019s capability of automated, reproducible and large-scale comparative experiments. The experimental results demonstrated that the CNN-LSTM model outperforms other models in extracting spatiotemporal correlation features from temperature time-series data, enabling high-precision prediction of multiple fire parameters. With a 6 s observation window and 10 m sensor spacing, the average R2 attains 0.942, an improvement of 2% over the baseline LSTM model, and the RMSE decreases by 28.8%. For sparse sensor deployment at 30 m spacing, the average R2 remains at 0.917, confirming the effectiveness of integrating spatial feature extraction with temporal modeling. This study provides an efficient technical pathway for intelligent tunnel fire identification and advances the research paradigm by shifting the traditional manual optimization process to a multi-agent system-based optimization workflow.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Fire"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5036294","title":["Multi-Agent Large Language Models As\u00a0Evolutionary Optimizers\u00a0For Scheduling Optimization"],"author":[{"given":"Yidan","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jiayin","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5036294","issued":{"date-parts":[[2024]]}},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.174612312.28926018\/v2","title":["Mathematical Framework for Large Language Models in Multi-Agent Systems for Interaction and Optimization"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-0241-1135","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Raman","family":"Marozau","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.174612312.28926018\/v2","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,9]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>This paper introduces an optimization framework centered on the minimization of the L-function, designed  to enhance the efficiency and contextual alignment of Large Language Models (LLMs) operating in MultiAgent Systems (MAS). The L-function integrates response brevity, historical context deviation, and taskspecific alignment into a unified objective, ensuring dynamic adaptability to diverse operational scenarios.  Key contributions include:  1) Theoretical derivation of the DH(O, H) and DT (O, T) metrics, rigorously quantifying historical and  task-based deviations using geometric and semantic embeddings.  2) Development of parameterized weighting factors (\u03b1, \u03b2, \u03b3, \u03bb) that dynamically adjust to task priorities,  system load, and resource availability, ensuring robustness in both standard and exceptional conditions.  3) Validation of the L-function\u2019s coherence and robustness, highlighting its efficacy in scenarios with noisy  embeddings or unforeseen task requirements.  Experimental directions are proposed to empirically validate the theoretical framework across diverse  MAS scenarios, focusing on scalability, contextual precision, and system efficiency. By embedding L into  MAS, the framework enables real-time adaptability, optimizing task distribution and resource utilization  across diverse applications, including finance, healthcare, and autonomous systems. This work bridges the  gap between theoretical optimization models and practical deployment in LLM-driven MAS, providing a  foundational step toward next-generation intelligent systems.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/case59546.2024.10711432","title":["Large Language Model-Enabled Multi-Agent Manufacturing Systems"],"author":[{"given":"Jonghan","family":"Lim","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Pennsylvania State University,Department of Industrial and Manufacturing and the Department of Mechanical Engineering,State College,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Birgit","family":"Vogel-Heuser","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Technical University of Munich,Institute of Automation and Information Systems,Munich,Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ilya","family":"Kovalenko","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Pennsylvania State University,Department of Industrial and Manufacturing and the Department of Mechanical Engineering,State College,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/case59546.2024.10711432","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,8,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,8,28]]},"container-title":["2024 IEEE 20th International Conference on Automation Science and Engineering (CASE)"]},{"DOI":"10.2196\/preprints.69742","title":["Advantages and Inconveniences of a Multi-Agent Large Language Model System to Mitigate Cognitive Biases in Diagnostic Challenges (Preprint)"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9775-2476","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Cedric","family":"Bousquet","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-8498-6150","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Div\u00e0","family":"Beltramin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2196\/preprints.69742","issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,12,6]]},"abstract":"<sec>\n                    <title>UNSTRUCTURED<\/title>\n                        <p>\u00a0<\/p>\n                <\/sec>"},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.174612312.28926018\/v1","title":["Mathematical Framework for Large Language Models in Multi-Agent Systems for Interaction and Optimization"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-0241-1135","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Raman","family":"Marozau","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.174612312.28926018\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,1]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>This paper introduces an optimization framework centered on the minimization of the L-function, designed  to enhance the efficiency and contextual alignment of Large Language Models (LLMs) operating in MultiAgent Systems (MAS). The L-function integrates response brevity, historical context deviation, and taskspecific alignment into a unified objective, ensuring dynamic adaptability to diverse operational scenarios.  Key contributions include:  1) Theoretical derivation of the DH(O, H) and DT (O, T) metrics, rigorously quantifying historical and  task-based deviations using geometric and semantic embeddings.  2) Development of parameterized weighting factors (\u03b1, \u03b2, \u03b3, \u03bb) that dynamically adjust to task priorities,  system load, and resource availability, ensuring robustness in both standard and exceptional conditions.  3) Validation of the L-function\u2019s coherence and robustness, highlighting its efficacy in scenarios with noisy  embeddings or unforeseen task requirements.  Experimental directions are proposed to empirically validate the theoretical framework across diverse  MAS scenarios, focusing on scalability, contextual precision, and system efficiency. By embedding L into  MAS, the framework enables real-time adaptability, optimizing task distribution and resource utilization  across diverse applications, including finance, healthcare, and autonomous systems. This work bridges the  gap between theoretical optimization models and practical deployment in LLM-driven MAS, providing a  foundational step toward next-generation intelligent systems.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6682038","title":["Multi-Agent Large Language Models with Reinforcement Learning for Quant Trading"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-4679-6039","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Qizhao","family":"Chen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Hiroaki","family":"Kawashima","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6682038","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The growing complexity and non-stationarity of financial markets make it increasingly difficult for traditional quantitative models to design robust trading strategies. Manually constructed alpha signals tend to exhibit limited adaptability when market conditions change, while machine learning-based approaches frequently lack interpretability and generalization across assets. To solve these issues, this research explores an alternative approach in which large language models (LLMs) are incorporated into the alpha design and portfolio weighting process. Specifically, we introduce a novel framework that integrates a multi-agent LLM system with reinforcement learning to improve formulaic alpha generation and portfolio allocation. In our setup, DeepSeek serves as the generation agent to construct a diverse pool of formulaic alphas, while Gemini acts as the evaluation agent to filter and select the most promising candidates. The selected alphas are then incorporated into a Deep Deterministic Policy Gradient (DDPG) framework, which optimizes their portfolio weights to maximize risk-adjusted returns. Empirical evaluations across seven major companies reveal that the multi-agent LLM + DDPG framework generally achieves higher cumulative returns than equal-weighted benchmarks and multi-agent PPO, though often with increased short-term risk. In contrast, single-agent PPO offers more stable, risk-controlled performance with smaller drawdowns. These results demonstrate that integrating LLM-generated signals with reinforcement learning can balance alpha exploitation and risk management, enabling adaptive and effective portfolio strategies across diverse market conditions.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.20944\/preprints202607.0981.v1","title":["Compliant Mechanism Design Using Multi-Agent Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Christian","family":"Spreafico","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Daniele","family":"Landi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Davide","family":"Russo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.20944\/preprints202607.0981.v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,14]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The transition from rigid-body to compliant mechanisms offers well-recognized advantages in mechanical design, including reduced part count, improved energy efficiency, and expanded functional integration. However, existing catalogue-based and artificial intelligence\u2013assisted design approaches often rely on abstract functional archetypes, limiting practical applicability and hindering identification of integrated, application-specific compliant solutions. This study presents a methodological framework for extracting compliant mechanism solutions from patent literature based on a structured, multi-agent pipeline employing Large Language Models (LLMs) within a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) architecture. The proposed approach integrates product decomposition, lexical and technological expansion, patent retrieval, compatibility assessment, and taxonomy-grounded classification using compliant mechanism archetypes. The framework is demonstrated through a case study involving 40 patents related to pedal-actuated waste bin mechanisms, enabling qualitative evaluation against expert judgment. The results indicate consistent identification of compliant alternatives to conventional rigid mechanisms, ranging from localized compliant substitutions (e.g., living hinges and leaf springs) to more integrated compliant transmission architectures. The patent-derived solutions provide application-specific structural embodiments, including geometric details and implementation information suitable for design reuse. Rather than proposing a finalized design tool, the study clarifies the architectural and methodological requirements for leveraging patent data in compliant mechanism design. By grounding design exploration in validated prior art and taxonomy-based reasoning, the proposed framework supports systematic discovery of contextually relevant compliant mechanisms while reducing abstraction and cognitive load in early-stage design.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-10302085\/v1","title":["A Multi-Agent Framework for Explainable Prompt Injection Detection in Large Language Models"],"author":[{"family":"Atul","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"allenhouse institute of technology kanpur"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-10302085\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,10]]},"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>\n                  Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in conversational agents, decision-support systems, software development, and autonomous AI applications. Despite their advantages, LLMs remain vulnerable to prompt injection attacks, where carefully crafted inputs alter model behavior, override security boundaries, or trigger unintended outputs. Existing defenses based on rules, keyword matching, or conventional machine learning often suffer from limited generalization and weak interpretability. This paper presents\n                  <bold>PromptShield<\/bold>\n                  , an interpretable framework for prompt injection detection that combines transformer-based semantic representation with explainable artificial intelligence (XAI). The proposed framework includes contextual feature extraction, attack classification, and post-hoc decision explanation using SHAP and LIME. PromptShield is evaluated using public prompt injection and jailbreak benchmarks with standard classification metrics, including accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and ROC-AUC.\n                <\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.52202\/085713-3638","title":["Multi-agent KTO: Enhancing Strategic Interactions of Large Language Model in Language Game"],"author":[{"given":"Rong","family":"Ye","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Bytedance"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yongxin","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"ByteDance Inc."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"yikai","family":"zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Haoyu","family":"Kuang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Fudan University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"peng","family":"sun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Tencent AI Lab"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"zhongyu","family":"wei","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.52202\/085713-3638","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 38"]},{"DOI":"10.4018\/979-8-3373-1419-8.ch003","title":["LLM-Based Multi-Agent Systems"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-1385-7031","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Ravi","family":"Sharma","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Bennett University, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dinesh","family":"Sahu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Bennett University, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Rajnish","family":"Chaturvedi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Bennett University, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4018\/979-8-3373-1419-8.ch003","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,25]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Large language model based multi agent system (LLM based MAS) is undoubtedly the most promising approaches towards artificial intelligence (AI). To build MAS, researchers utilize two major capabilities of LLMs viz. logical reasoning and effective planning. The existing literature related to MAS is indeed scattered and there is an urgent need to collect the information at one place to gain deeper insights. With this motivation this chapter was designed with the primary focus on providing a detailed overview of LLM based MAS. The chapter is divided into five major sections; construction of LLM-MAS, its key capabilities, its current applications with detailed examples, intrinsic limitations and challenges and finally a brief discussion about future trends in LLM based MAS research. Through this chapter we want our readers to gain a better understanding of this technological advancement which will enable us to unlock unseen opportunities in various public sectors.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Advances in Computational Intelligence and Robotics","Advancements in Multi-Agent Large Language Model Systems for Next-Generation AI"]},{"DOI":"10.4018\/979-8-3373-1419-8.ch009","title":["Security and Privacy in Multi-Agent LLM Networks"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3601-102X","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Pawan Kumar","family":"Goel","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Raj Kumar Goel Institute of Technology, Ghaziabad, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2634-5600","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Satya Prakash","family":"Yadav","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Madan Mohan Malaviya University of Technology, Gorakhpur, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Monika","family":"Singh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Bharat Institute of Technology, Meerut, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4018\/979-8-3373-1419-8.ch009","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,25]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The integration of multi-agent systems with large language models (LLMs) is a significant area in artificial intelligence, enabling collaborative problem-solving and decision-making. However, these networks pose significant security and privacy vulnerabilities, making them crucial for research and practical applications. This chapter focuses on vulnerability mitigation in multi-agent LLM networks, focusing on adversarial attacks, data leakage, and unauthorized access. Existing literature highlights challenges such as lack of robust defense mechanisms, complexity of securing decentralized communication, and privacy preservation in shared learning environments. A novel framework is proposed that integrates adaptive encryption, differential privacy, and decentralized consensus protocols to address these vulnerabilities. Experimental results show a 40% improvement in attack detection accuracy and a 35% reduction in data leakage compared to existing methods.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Advances in Computational Intelligence and Robotics","Advancements in Multi-Agent Large Language Model Systems for Next-Generation AI"]},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0014480400004061","title":["Evaluating the Effectiveness of Multi-Agent Large Language Models for Automated Vulnerable Code Repair"],"author":[{"given":"Martin","family":"Kilgi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Software Science, Tallinn University of Technology, Tallinn, Estonia"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Hayretdin","family":"Bahsi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Software Science, Tallinn University of Technology, Tallinn, Estonia"},{"name":"School of Informatics, Computing, and Cyber Systems, Northern Arizona University, U.S.A."}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0014480400004061","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 12th International Conference on Information Systems Security and Privacy"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6192518","title":["Language-Driven Coordination in Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Benjamin J.","family":"Wilson","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Oliver","family":"Smith","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Chloe A.","family":"Thompson","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6192518","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>This study integrates LLMs into MARL through language-based coordination, communication, and memory modules. The framework combines proximal policy optimization (PPO) with language-conditioned subgoal generation. Experiments across three multi-agent environments involving over 10,000 training episodes show a 14.7-26.3% increase in win rate compared with MAPPO and QMIX baselines, with ablation results confirming the contribution of language-guided coordination to sample efficiency and zero-shot generalization.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5078120","title":["Dynamic Multi-Agent Orchestration and Retrieval for Multi-Source Question-Answer Systems using Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Antony","family":"Medeiros","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Claudio","family":"Cavalcante","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5078120","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]}},{"DOI":"10.52202\/085713-4904","title":["MASTER: Enhancing Large Language Model via Multi-Agent Simulated Teaching"],"author":[{"given":"Liang","family":"Yue","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Harbin Institute of Technology, Shenzhen"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yihong","family":"Tang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Harbin Institute of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Kehai","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Harbin Institute of Technology (Shenzhen)"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jie","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"The Chinese University of Hong Kong"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Min","family":"zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Harbin Institute of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.52202\/085713-4904","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 38"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/zinc69910.2026.11655671","title":["Multi-Agent Large Language Model Based Emotional Detoxification Through Personalized Intensity Control for Consumer Protection"],"author":[{"given":"Keito","family":"Inoshita","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Kansai University","place":["Suita, Japan"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/zinc69910.2026.11655671","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"container-title":["2026 IEEE Zooming Innovation in Consumer Technologies International Conference (ZINC)"]},{"DOI":"10.64898\/2026.06.19.26355815","title":["PHO-Agents: A Large Language Model\u2013Powered Multi-Agent System for Predicting Health Outcomes"],"author":[{"given":"Daling","family":"Shi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Health Outcomes and Biomedical Informatics, University of Florida, FL, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tyler","family":"Shugg","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Division of Clinical Pharmacology, Department of Medicine, Indiana University School of Medicine, IN, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Michael T.","family":"Eadon","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Medicine, Indiana University School of Medicine, IN, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4917-6173","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jing","family":"Su","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Biostatistics and Health Data Science, Indiana University School of Medicine, IN, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yijiang","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Radiation Oncology, School of medicine, Stanford university, Palo Alto, CA, USA"},{"name":"Department of Medicine, College of Medicine, University of Florida, Gainesville, FL, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4455-5302","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Qianqian","family":"Song","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Health Outcomes and Biomedical Informatics, University of Florida, FL, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.64898\/2026.06.19.26355815","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,29]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>ABSTRACT<\/jats:title>\n                <jats:sec>\n                  <jats:title>Objective<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>Predicting health outcomes from electronic health records (EHRs) is challenging because traditional models rely on structured data and often ignore external medical knowledge. We propose an approach that integrates structured EHR with text-based clinical evidence to improve prediction and interpretability.<\/jats:p>\n                <\/jats:sec>\n                <jats:sec>\n                  <jats:title>Methods<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>We introduce PHO-Agents, a multi-agent system powered by large language models (LLMs) for health outcome prediction. Structured EHR sequences are encoded to produce attention-based representations and initial logits, which are converted into patient summaries by a data agent. A retrieval agent gathers relevant clinical guidelines. Research and practical doctor agents independently assess the patient, and a leader agent synthesizes their analyses. Outputs from the EHR-based model and the LLM agents are fused to generate final predictions and explanation reports. PHO-Agents was evaluated on three real-world cohorts: acute kidney injury (AKI) patients (in-hospital mortality), chronic kidney disease patients (AKI onset within two years), and cancer patients receiving immune checkpoint inhibitors (immune-related adverse events within one year).<\/jats:p>\n                <\/jats:sec>\n                <jats:sec>\n                  <jats:title>Results<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>PHO-Agents outperformed single-agent and multi-agent LLM baselines across all cohorts. In the AKI mortality task, it achieved a PR-AUC of 90.20 \u00b1 2.07, compared with 56.46 \u00b1 2.98 for the best single-agent baseline. Similar gains were observed in the ICI and CKD cohorts. Ablation studies showed that both multi-agent reasoning and logit-level fusion contributed to performance improvements, and case analyses demonstrated clinically consistent explanations.<\/jats:p>\n                <\/jats:sec>\n                <jats:sec>\n                  <jats:title>Conclusion<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>PHO-Agents integrates longitudinal EHR modeling with collaborative LLM reasoning, improving predictive performance, interpretability, and robustness across diverse clinical tasks. This hybrid approach offers a trustworthy strategy for real-world clinical decision support.<\/jats:p>\n                <\/jats:sec>"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/isssr61934.2024.00028","title":["Research on Multi-agent Path Planning Algorithm Based on Large Language Model"],"author":[{"given":"Liu","family":"Lian","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Dalian Naval Academy,Operation Software and Simulation Research Institute"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Cui","family":"Peng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Dalian Naval Academy,Operation Software and Simulation Research Institute"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/isssr61934.2024.00028","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,3,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,3,16]]},"container-title":["2024 10th International Symposium on System Security, Safety, and Reliability (ISSSR)"]},{"DOI":"10.3390\/su18073554","title":["HCEA: A Multi-Agent Framework for Sustainable Human-Centered Entrepreneurship Based on a Large Language Model"],"author":[{"given":"Yu","family":"Gao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Economics and Management, Yanbian University, Yanji 133002, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4463-1053","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yanji","family":"Piao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Economics and Management, Yanbian University, Yanji 133002, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dongzhe","family":"Xuan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Engineering, Yanbian University, Yanji 133002, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/su18073554","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,4]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Human-centered entrepreneurship considers employee well-being and uses the Sustainable Development Goals as its fundamental pillars. However, existing research predominantly focuses on institutional interventions and fails to provide integrated intelligent solutions for tackling human\u2013machine collaboration issues in the context of digital transformation. Large language models (LLMs) offer potential for affective computing and personalized support, but face critical gaps in ethical governance, privacy protection, and real-time risk intervention in sensitive entrepreneurial contexts. Our proposed Human-Centered Entrepreneurial Intelligent Agent (HCEA) framework achieves the unified optimization of task utility, empathetic expression, and ethical security by integrating a large language model core fine-tuned via a multi-objective hybrid loss function and a cluster of task-specialized intelligent agents. HCEA integrates retrieval-enhanced generation to ensure suggestion accuracy, a hierarchical data governance system for sensitivity-based privacy protection, and an independent risk detection module for real-time intervention and referral. We build the framework by constructing a hybrid entrepreneurial dataset, design the multi-agent architecture of decision support, emotion understanding and ethical risk tracking, and empirically evaluate both comparisons and ablation experiments. The results demonstrate that HCEA outperforms five baseline models across six key metrics, including entrepreneurship guidance relevance, emotion recognition, and high-risk recall. This study contributes to the intersection of digital transformation and sustainable entrepreneurship by providing a technically feasible, ethically grounded intelligent framework that empowers enterprises to reconcile efficiency with human-centric values, advancing SDG 8 (decent work and economic growth) and SDG 9 (industry, innovation, and infrastructure).<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Sustainability"]},{"DOI":"10.1038\/s41598-026-44206-z","title":["Public opinion dissemination simulation based on large language model multi-agent systems"],"author":[{"given":"Hai","family":"Lan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Hua","family":"Hu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Peng Cheng","family":"Guo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Qi","family":"Huang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1038\/s41598-026-44206-z","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,4]]},"container-title":["Scientific Reports"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/medcom67532.2025.11405392","title":["Large Language Model-enabled Multi-Agent Manufacturing Systems"],"author":[{"given":"Abhay","family":"Kumar Pandey","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"IEC College of Engineering &#x0026; Technology,Dept. of CSE,Greater Noida,India,201310"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Vipin","family":"Kr. Kushwaha","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"IEC College of Engineering &#x0026; Technology,Dept. of CSE\/IT,Greater Noida,India,201310"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shiva","family":"Gupta","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"IEC College of Engineering &#x0026; Technology,Dept. of CSE,Greater Noida,India,201310"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"B.","family":"Sharan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"IEC College of Engineering &#x0026; Technology,Dept. of CSE,Greater Noida,201310"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/medcom67532.2025.11405392","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,11]]},"container-title":["2025 Modern Electronics Devices and Intelligent Communication Systems (MEDCOM)"]},{"DOI":"10.20944\/preprints202512.2748.v1","title":["Contextual Trust Evaluation for Robust Coordination in Large Language Model Multi-Agent Systems"],"author":[{"given":"Kangning","family":"Gao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Haotian","family":"Zhu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Rui","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jinming","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Xu","family":"Yan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yi","family":"Hu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.20944\/preprints202512.2748.v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,31]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Large Language Model (LLM)-based multi-agent systems have emerged as a promising paradigm for tackling complex tasks that exceed individual agent capabilities. However, existing approaches often suffer from coordination inefficiencies, a lack of trust mechanisms, and suboptimal role assignment strategies. This paper presents a novel trust-aware coordination framework that enhances multi-agent collaboration through dynamic role assignment and context sharing. Our framework introduces a multi-dimensional trust evaluation mechanism that continuously assesses agent reliability based on performance history, interaction quality, and behavioral consistency. The coordinator leverages these trust scores to dynamically assign roles and orchestrate agent interactions while maintaining a shared context repository for transparent information exchange. We evaluate our framework across eight diverse task scenarios with varying complexity levels, demonstrating significant improvements over baseline approaches. Experimental results show that our trust-aware framework achieves a 87.4% task success rate, reducing execution time by 36.3% compared to non-trust-based methods, while maintaining 43.2% lower communication overhead. The framework's ability to adapt agent roles based on evolving trust scores enables more efficient resource utilization and robust fault tolerance in dynamic multi-agent environments.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.54844\/vte.2025.0921","title":["Development of vocational education and training chatbot supported by large language model-based multi-agent system"],"author":[{"given":"Yuefan","family":"Yu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Zhiqun","family":"Zhao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.54844\/vte.2025.0921","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,8,8]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Vocational education and training\u00a0(VET) chatbots face issues such as difficulties in competence development support, limitations in teaching decision-making, and defects in competence assessment methods. This study constructed an large language model\u00a0(LLM)-based VET chatbot and designed a human-computer collaborative teaching model. It applied a quasi-experimental design to conduct a teaching experiment in a course titled Electronic Circuits and CAD\u00a0Plate Making at a VET institution. Results showed that the LLM-based\u00a0VET chatbot and its application model could significantly improve students' Level 1 and Level 2 professional competence\u00a0(functional competence and processual competence according to COMET).\u00a0It also discusses ideas for future optimization of the LLM-based VET chatbot.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Vocation, Technology &amp; Education"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/ijcnn64981.2025.11228910","title":["Dream2Drive: A Large Language Model Powered Agent for Real World Multi-task Vehicle Motion Planning"],"author":[{"given":"Hongru","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"East China Normal University,School of Computer Science and Technology,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jun","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"East China Normal University,School of Computer Science and Technology,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/ijcnn64981.2025.11228910","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,6,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,6,30]]},"container-title":["2025 International Joint Conference on Neural Networks (IJCNN)"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.imu.2026.101783","title":["Agent role structure and operating characteristics in large language model clinical classification: A comparative study of specialist and deliberative multi-agent protocols"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-7858-1575","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Callum","family":"Anderson","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.imu.2026.101783","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"container-title":["Informatics in Medicine Unlocked"]},{"DOI":"10.31219\/osf.io\/nbm4f","title":["CoordiLang: Assessing Multi-Agent Coordination Skills in Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Emma","family":"Taylor","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jacob","family":"Lee","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.31219\/osf.io\/nbm4f","issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,12,12]]},"abstract":"<p>The advanced reasoning and inferential capabilities exhibited by Large Language Models (LLMs) position them as viable candidates for orchestrating coordination among multiple agents. This paper presents \\textit{CoordiLang}, a novel benchmark designed to rigorously evaluate the coordination prowess of LLMs within the framework of Pure Coordination Games, where agents must collaborate without conflicting interests to maximize collective gains. \\textit{CoordiLang} encompasses two primary evaluation facets: (1) \\textbf{Agentic Coordination}, wherein LLMs assume proactive roles in facilitating cooperation across four distinct pure coordination scenarios; and (2) \\textbf{Coordination Question Answering (QA)}, involving 200 meticulously crafted multiple-choice queries derived from the aforementioned games to assess three critical reasoning dimensions: Environmental Understanding, Theory of Mind (ToM) Reasoning, and Collaborative Planning. Additionally, we introduce the \\textit{Coordination Cognitive Framework (CCF)}, a modular architecture enabling seamless integration of various LLMs as interchangeable components within coordination tasks. Empirical results demonstrate that LLMs, particularly those augmented with the latest iterations like GPT-4-X, achieve performance on par with state-of-the-art reinforcement learning (RL) agents in environments necessitating intuitive, environment-based actions. Notably, zero-shot coordination assessments reveal that LLMs exhibit enhanced adaptability to novel partners compared to traditional RL methodologies. However, significant gaps remain in their ToM reasoning and collaborative planning capabilities, highlighting avenues for future improvement. Our comprehensive analysis underscores the pivotal role of environmental comprehension and partner intention inference in effective multi-agent collaboration.<\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.32614\/cran.package.mini007","title":["mini007: Lightweight Framework for Orchestrating Multi-Agent Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Mohamed El Fodil","family":"Ihaddaden","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.32614\/cran.package.mini007","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,5]]},"container-title":["CRAN: Contributed Packages"]},{"DOI":"10.4018\/979-8-3373-1419-8.ch005","title":["Multimodal Data Processing and Multi-Agent Systems"],"author":[{"given":"L.","family":"Namitha","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"VIT Bhopal University, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"P. R.","family":"Buvaneswari","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"VIT Bhopal University, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4018-1208","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"D.","family":"Lakshmi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"VIT Bhopal University, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4018\/979-8-3373-1419-8.ch005","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,25]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>As technology progresses rapidly, the integration of large language models (LLMs), multimodal data processing, and multi-agent systems (MAS) is turning various domains from healthcare into autonomous systems. This chapter provides a comprehensive discovery of these major regions with their fundamental concepts, architecture, processing mechanisms, and real-world applications. We examine the role of MAS in intelligent decision-making, complications of multimodal data fusion, and the developed capabilities of LLM in handling diverse information currents, and we analyze the challenges associated with these techniques, in which the interpretation, scalability, and prejudice, including mitigation, identify the emerging opportunities running innovation. Future trends such as neurosymbolic AI, edge computing, self-levied learning and multi-agent reinforcement are also discovered, providing insight into the intelligent and adaptive systems of the next generation. This chapter serves as a wide guide for navigating the developed landscape of multimodal AI and MAS.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Advances in Computational Intelligence and Robotics","Advancements in Multi-Agent Large Language Model Systems for Next-Generation AI"]},{"DOI":"10.4018\/979-8-3373-1419-8.ch007","title":["Sustainability in Multi-Agent LLM System"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3601-102X","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Pawan Kumar","family":"Goel","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Raj Kumar Goel Institute of Technology, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2634-5600","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Satya Prakash","family":"Yadav","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Madan Mohan Malaviya University of Technology, Gorakhpur, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9257-9181","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Prashant","family":"Upadhyay","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Sharda University, Greater Noida, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4018\/979-8-3373-1419-8.ch007","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,25]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The rapid growth of artificial intelligence (AI) has led to significant energy consumption and environmental impact in multi-agent large language model (LLM) systems. To ensure the long-term viability of AI advancements while minimizing their carbon footprint, research is focused on optimizing energy efficiency in these systems. This chapter proposes a novel framework that leverages adaptive agent collaboration and energy-aware scheduling algorithms to reduce energy usage without compromising system performance. The approach introduces a dynamic load-balancing mechanism that distributes tasks among agents based on real-time energy availability and computational demand. Experimental results show a 30-40% reduction in energy consumption compared to traditional systems, while maintaining comparable accuracy and response times. This breakthrough represents a significant advancement in sustainable AI.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Advances in Computational Intelligence and Robotics","Advancements in Multi-Agent Large Language Model Systems for Next-Generation AI"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6845148","title":["Large Language Model-Enhanced Feedback for Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning-Based Optimal Control of Data Center Cooling"],"author":[{"given":"Long","family":"Deng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Na","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zengxi","family":"Feng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Pei","family":"Cao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Anjun","family":"Zhao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dingxing","family":"Hu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6845148","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Data center cooling is a major source of non-IT energy consumption, and optimal cooling control must reduce power consumption while maintaining safe rack inlet air temperatures. To address insufficient state representation, credit-assignment difficulty, and limited reward expressiveness in strongly coupled cooling control, this paper proposes LEF-MARL, a large-language-model-enhanced multi-agent reinforcement learning framework. The cooling task is decomposed into three cooperative agents, and coupling, dynamic, and power-related information is incorporated into local observations. A domain-knowledge-prompted large language model is used as an offline preference annotator to express high-level operational criteria, including thermal safety, energy efficiency, and cooling-loop coordination, as pairwise comparisons of candidate operating states. These comparisons are converted into continuous state scores using the Thurstone-Mosteller model, and the learned scores are further used to construct preference-score difference rewards, providing dense and agent-attributable learning signals without online LLM inference. A simulation environment is developed using a high-density AI data center prototype, real operational data, and meteorological data. Compared with four baselines across seven representative operating conditions, LEF-MARL achieves the lowest average cooling power and average PUE, namely 42.36 kW and 1.233, while maintaining an average rack inlet air temperature of 24.48 \u00b0C without temperature violations. It reduces average cooling power by 19.32% over rule-based control and by 2.93% over the best learning-based baseline, while eliminating residual overheating violations. The ablation results support the effectiveness of dynamically coupled state representation and the proposed preference-score difference reward construction mechanism.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.4018\/979-8-3373-1419-8","title":["Advancements in Multi-Agent Large Language Model Systems for Next-Generation AI"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4018\/979-8-3373-1419-8","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,25]]},"container-title":["Advances in Computational Intelligence and Robotics"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6582218","title":["Standardizing Road Traffic Safety Facility Operation and Maintenance: A Multi-Agent Large Language Model Approach"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-0080-4688","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Chaoxian","family":"Yang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-8196-6128","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Xiaomin","family":"Huang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4223-8186","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Zhiyi","family":"Tang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3032-2839","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Mengmeng","family":"Sun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-9901-9423","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Wei","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-3836-775X","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Panping","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-5958-9288","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Wei","family":"Xu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6582218","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>With the rapid expansion of road networks, there is an increasingly urgent demand for intelligent management of Traffic Safety Facilities (TSFs). Current operation and maintenance practices still rely heavily on manual expertise, resulting in low efficiency and a high risk of human error. Although large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated strong reasoning capabilities, their application to TSF-related tasks is constrained by limited domain knowledge and delayed knowledge updates. To address these challenges, this study proposes a knowledge-base-enhanced multi-agent collaborative reasoning approach for TSF-related tasks. A two-stage knowledge modeling strategy is developed to transform heterogeneous and regulation-intensive source knowledge into hierarchical knowledge units with semantic fingerprints, thereby enabling structured knowledge storage and efficient retrieval. On this basis, a multi-agent collaborative framework is further constructed to support intelligent task routing, hierarchical query augmentation, dense-sparse collaborative retrieval, multidimensional re-ranking, and iterative self-reflective generation. Meanwhile, a TSF question-answering (QA) benchmark is established, and systematic comparative experiments are conducted on closed-ended detail discrimination, open-ended comprehensive generation, and representative case analysis against original open-source LLMs, Naive Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), Light RAG, and state-of-the-art closed-source models. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed approach achieves superior performance in factual accuracy, semantic consistency, response completeness, and regulatory compliance, while effectively mitigating hallucinations without requiring large-scale fine-tuning. The proposed method provides a promising technical pathway for intelligent TSF operation and maintenance as well as engineering decision support.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-9300129\/v1","title":["A Multi-Agent Large Language Model Framework for Marketing Decision-Making with Auditable Attribution Analysis"],"author":[{"given":"Qianxi","family":"Liu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of South Florida"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Hengguang","family":"Cui","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Brown University"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yingzi","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Minnesota, Twin Cities"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Chi Shing","family":"Lee","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Hunter College"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Sheng","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Northeastern University"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Linghao","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Chicago"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-9300129\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,3]]},"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>\n                  <italic>This paper addresses the challenges of inconsistent indicator definitions, complex attribution paths, and difficulty in tracing decision-making criteria in multi-channel marketing environments. It proposes a multi-agent large-scale model framework for marketing decision-making, constructing an end-to-end analysis loop around problem parsing, definition alignment, and auditable output. The system consists of a problem agent, an Metric Agent, and a decision agent: the problem agent maps natural language requirements into structured task specifications, clarifying target indicators, time windows, analysis dimensions, and constraint sets; the Metric Agent performs field mapping and indicator standardization on multi-source event data, unifying window filtering, deduplication rules, and cross-platform merging strategies, and providing validity judgments and consistent aggregation criteria for fine-grained slices; the decision agent generates executable resource allocation suggestions under budget and business constraints, and simultaneously outputs a recalculated evidence chain, ensuring the traceability of suggestion sources, calculation paths, and definition versions. Comparative experimental results show that the proposed method maintains strong robustness and reliable delivery capabilities under a multi-dimensional evaluation system, particularly excelling in constraint satisfaction, verifiable output, and auditable coverage. It provides an interpretable, traceable, and implementable decision support solution for marketing attribution assessment and ROI optimization.<\/italic>\n                <\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7231715","title":["From Runnable to Behaviorally Reliable Models: A Multi-Agent Large Language Model Framework for Automated Modelica Modeling and Repair"],"author":[{"given":"Shutong","family":"Feng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jiasheng","family":"Cheng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ganpeng","family":"He","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jianxiang","family":"Zheng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7377-1242","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Yaoli","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7231715","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Engineering simulation modeling is a critical engineering activity, yet constructing reliable equation-based simulation models remains labor-intensive and requires substantial domain expertise. Although large language models (LLMs) can generate simulation code from natural-language requirements, generated models may compile and execute successfully while still violating engineering requirements, physical assumptions, or control objectives.This paper proposes a multi-agent LLM framework for automated Modelica modeling, simulation, and behavioral repair. The AI contribution is a closed-loop workflow that combines Modelica code generation, OpenModelica-based execution feedback, domain-aware review, and iterative repair. The engineering application is automated equation-based simulation modeling, where natural-language requirements are transformed into executable and behaviorally reliable Modelica models.Five specialized reviewer agents evaluate parameter consistency, component semantics, equation physicality, control logic, and result behavior to identify hidden errors beyond compilation and simulation success. The framework is evaluated on 20 representative Modelica modeling tasks. Using execution-feedback-based repair alone, all generated models become runnable, but only 16 of 20 achieve correct simulation results and only 8 of 20 satisfy critical behavioral correctness requirements. After multi-agent review and iterative repair, all 20 tasks achieve correct simulation results and critical behavioral correctness. Reviewer-removal experiments further demonstrate the value of specialized reviewers for improving the reliability of AI-generated engineering simulation software.Keywords: Large language models; Multi-agent systems; Modelica; Engineering simulation modeling; Software verification and validation; Behavioral repair<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.autcon.2025.106331","title":["Multi-agent large language model framework for code-compliant automated design of reinforced concrete structures"],"author":[{"given":"Jinxin","family":"Chen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yi","family":"Bao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.autcon.2025.106331","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,9]]},"container-title":["Automation in Construction"]},{"DOI":"10.35542\/osf.io\/etv6d_v1","title":["Can Large Language Models Generate Role-Consistent Clinical Dialogue for Education? A Multi-agent Approach"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6524-9382","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"David","family":"Power","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.35542\/osf.io\/etv6d_v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,23]]},"abstract":"<p>Background: The development of high-quality healthcare simulation scenarios and educationalclinical dialogue is resource-intensive, limiting scalability in healthcare education. Large languagemodels (LLMs) offer new opportunities for generating dynamic conversational simulations but raiseconcerns regarding role fidelity, realism, and evaluation.Innovation: We describe a role-locked, multi-agent LLM system designed to generate realisticemergency department conversations involving a doctor, nurse, and patient. Separate LLM agentswere assigned fixed clinical roles and interacted within a shared conversational environment, supportedby explicit role constraints and rule-based role-guarding mechanisms. An independent LLM wasused as an automated evaluator (\u201cAI-as-judge\u201d) to assess role fidelity, turn coherence, communicationrealism, and educational usability.Evaluation: Twenty-five simulated conversations were generated and evaluated using the automatedjudge. A subset of ten conversations underwent independent human evaluation by two clinically experienced raters using aligned assessment domains. Automated evaluation demonstrated consistentlyhigh ratings across all domains, with all simulations judged educationally usable. Human evaluation showed substantial agreement for role fidelity and moderate agreement across other domains,providing expert plausibility benchmarking for the automated approach.Implications: This work demonstrates the feasibility of a role-locked, multi-agent LLM architecturefor generating educationally plausible conversational simulations. The combination of automated andlimited human evaluation provides early validity evidence supporting feasibility and usability. Thisapproach may support rapid prototyping and scalable development of simulation-based educationalcontent.<\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.22541\/au.176025089.91562731\/v1","title":["Research on Multi-Agent Competition Based on Large Language Models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1278-671X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jibin","family":"Yin","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Kunming University of Science and Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-8938-0899","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jiang","family":"Mulin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Kunming University of Science and Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-7018-2353","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Hong","family":"Qiuhong","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Kunming University of Science and Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zhang","family":"Xiangliang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Zhejiang University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.22541\/au.176025089.91562731\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,12]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Large Language Models (LLMs), with their advanced capabilities in\nsemantic understanding, dynamic strategy generation, and complex\nbehavior simulation, represent a transformative tool for studying\nmulti-agent interactions. Their exceptional performance in areas such as\nnatural language processing and social behavior modeling enables the\nsimulation of socio-economic phenomena through LLM-driven agents.\nHowever, existing research has primarily focused on agent collaboration,\nleaving a significant gap in the exploration of competition\nmechanisms an essential driver of social evolution and economic\ninnovation. To address this theoretical gap, this paper introduces a\nbidirectional competition-cooperation framework, where service agents\nand experience agents are treated as equal participants in a game. A\nthree-dimensional competitive space of \u201dresources-influence-information\u201d\nis constructed. Leveraging LLM-driven strategy generators and\nreinforcement learning optimization mechanisms, service agents can\ndynamically adjust strategies such as pricing and menus, while\nexperience agents influence market dynamics through behaviors such as\nsocial network dissemination and hidden information exploration. This\nresearch overcomes the unidirectional constraints of traditional models,\nhighlighting the symbiotic evolution of competition-cooperation\nbehaviors in digital societies. It also provides a computationally\nviable theoretical tool for applications such as platform economy\ngovernance and metaverse business simulation.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.172954477.70259514\/v1","title":["Multi-Agent Formation Control Using Large Language Models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-8259-0680","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yitong","family":"Liu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zihao","family":"Zhou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jiawen","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Liangming","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jiankun","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.172954477.70259514\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,10,21]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>We explore the innovative application of multimodal large language models (LLMs) in formation control of unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) swarms by using image and text as input. To validate the feasibility of using LLMs in UAVs, in the first stage, we pre-train a large language model (LLM) using a dataset of 5000 samples for a single UAV, achieving a success rate of 78.0% in command extraction. This demonstrates the feasibility of using LLMs to control UAVs. Subsequently, we extend this framework to a UAV swarm, where the dataset is expanded to 20,000 samples, and the swarm LLM is pre-trained using this dataset. We utilize the GPT-4 multimodal LLM to process images captured by the leading UAV, which provides the UAV swarm with the capabilities of environmental recognition and semantic understanding. The pre-trained model achieves a success rate of 82.7% in command extraction. Specifically, for image understanding using multimodal LLM, we innovatively employ GPT-4 to process images captured by a leading UAV in the swarm, enabling visual feature-based formation planning with a planning success rate of 83.8%. Fianlly, based on our real-world experiments, LLMs exhibit outstanding performance in addressing both individual and collective UAV planning problems, demonstrating the extensive potential application of LLMs in multi-agent formation control and collective swarm tasks. Our experimental video is uploaded to https:\/\/youtu.be\/WWQToKf8iyM.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-031-77367-9_29","title":["Beyond Chatbots: Enhancing Luxembourgish Language Learning Through Multi-agent Systems and\u00a0Large Language Model"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-6722-4008","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sana","family":"Nouzri","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Meryem","family":"EL Fatimi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Titouan","family":"Guerin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Mahfoud","family":"Othmane","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7784-6176","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Amro","family":"Najjar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-3-031-77367-9_29","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,15]]},"container-title":["Lecture Notes in Computer Science","PRIMA 2024: Principles and Practice of Multi-Agent Systems"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5242786","title":["Industry Chain Agent: Large Language Model-Based Agent for Intelligent Generation of Industry Chain Graph"],"author":[{"given":"Mengjia","family":"Du","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Guojun","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Qingsong","family":"Ruan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5242786","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/fllm67465.2025.11391246","title":["Evaluating Multi-Agent Defences Against Jailbreaking Attacks on Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Maria","family":"Carolina","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Luxembourg,Department of Computer Science,Esch-sur-Alzette,Luxembourg"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Cornelia","family":"Wit","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Luxembourg,Department of Computer Science,Esch-sur-Alzette,Luxembourg"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jun","family":"Pang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Luxembourg,Department of Computer Science,Esch-sur-Alzette,Luxembourg"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/fllm67465.2025.11391246","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,25]]},"container-title":["2025 3rd International Conference on Foundation and Large Language Models (FLLM)"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.aei.2025.104246","title":["AirfoilAgent: Airfoil aerodynamics optimization design via large language model multi-agent collaborations"],"author":[{"given":"Yi","family":"Fan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Hao","family":"Zhan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mingxuan","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Baigang","family":"Mi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.aei.2025.104246","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4]]},"container-title":["Advanced Engineering Informatics"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/uv63228.2024.11189149","title":["Multi-Agent Knowledge Graph Question Answering Framework Based on Lightweight Large Language Model"],"author":[{"given":"Donghai","family":"Zhang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Lenovo Knowdee (Beijing) Intelligent Technology,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Shuangtao","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Lenovo Knowdee (Beijing) Intelligent Technology,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Bo","family":"Fu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Lenovo Knowdee (Beijing) Intelligent Technology,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Shuo","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beijing University of Technology,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/uv63228.2024.11189149","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,10,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,10,19]]},"container-title":["2024 7th International Conference on Universal Village (UV)"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5362568","title":["Llm-Mtmp: A Large Language Model-Based Multi-Agent Task and Motion Planning Framework for Power Inspection Robots"],"author":[{"given":"Zongyuan","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Xin","family":"Zhou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jianliang","family":"Mao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Chuanlin","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Chenggang","family":"Cui","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jun","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5362568","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]}},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6277139","title":["Cognitive Heuristics are Necessary for Human-Like Multi-Agent Dynamics in Large Language Models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6424-2752","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Hongan","family":"Zhu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4964-8655","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Babak","family":"Heydari","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6277139","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly proposed as substitutes for human subjects in behavioral experiments, but it remains unclear whether they reproduce the bounded-rational heuristics that give rise to canonical dynamics in multi-agent systems. Traditional agent-based models require extensive empirical data and parameter calibration, while off-the-shelf LLMs often generate behaviors that diverge from human decision-making. In this work, we investigate whether LLMs, when guided by descriptive accounts of human cognition, can offer a practical middle ground. Our framework runs the Beer Distribution Game-a canonical testbed for bounded rationality in dynamic environments-with LLM players and evaluates their actions against an empirically calibrated cognitive model of human decision-making. We further train a neural network to decode latent decision parameters from LLM plays and map them into the parameter space estimated from human data. Results show that off-the-shelf LLMs poorly match human behavior. In contrast, prompts that explicitly describe the heuristic processes underlying human cognition yield game sessions statistically indistinguishable from those of human players. These findings are further supported by parameter inference and indicate that LLMs, when conditioned on descriptive cognitive priors, can approximate human decision-making without deliberate calibration. More broadly, our results suggest that LLMs do not endogenously exhibit misperceptions of feedback; bounded rationality must be explicitly supplied. LLMs thus offer a complement to traditional modeling approaches in behavioral and system dynamics research, but only when grounded in descriptive accounts of human cognition.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/wsc63780.2024.10838923","title":["Integrating Large Language Models into Agent Models for Multi-Agent Simulations: Preliminary Report"],"author":[{"given":"Hiromitsu","family":"Hattori","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Information Science and Engineering, Ritsumeikan University,Kyoto,JAPAN"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Arata","family":"Kato","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Graduate School of Information Science and Engineering, Ritsumeikan University,Kyoto,JAPAN"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mamoru","family":"Yoshizoe","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Information Science and Engineering, Ritsumeikan University,Kyoto,JAPAN"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/wsc63780.2024.10838923","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,12,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,12,15]]},"container-title":["2024 Winter Simulation Conference (WSC)"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,1]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Large Language Models (LLMs) have recently become widely used by private investors seeking personalized financial guidance. Yet emerging research indicates that such models systematically amplify several investment risks, including excessive geographic concentration, sector clustering, trend chasing, elevated active management exposure, and increased total expense ratios. At the same time, Multi-Agent LLM architectures have demonstrated notable improvements in quantitative financial analytics by integrating specialized reasoning components, structured workflows, and dynamic code execution. This paper combines these two streams of insight by introducing a novel Bias-Aware Multi-Agent LLM Framework designed specifically for retail investment advisory. The proposed system incorporates risk auditing, bias detection, regulatorystyle constraint enforcement, and user-centered explanation mechanisms into a Multi-Agent foundation. Experimental evaluations show that architecture substantially reduces key investment risks while preserving clarity, interpretability, and rigorous analytical performance. The work aims to move toward safer, more transparent, and more responsible AI systems for consumer-facing financial applications.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.21917\/ijms.2026.0355","title":["A BIASAWARE MULTI-AGENT LARGE LANGUAGE MODEL FRAMEWORK FOR SAFE AND EXPLAINABLE RETAIL INVESTMENT ADVISORY"],"author":[{"given":"Ojas","family":"Nimaje","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"BITS Pilani, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,1]]},"container-title":["ICTACT Journal on Management Studies"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,1]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21917\/ijms.2026.0355"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/iciibms66230.2025.11316762","title":["A Large Language Model-Driven Multi-Agent Human-Machine Interaction Framework"],"author":[{"given":"Libowen","family":"Wu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Shanghai Institute of Technology,Faculty of Intelligence Technology,Shanghai,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Huailin","family":"Zhao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Shanghai Institute of Technology,Faculty of Intelligence Technology,Shanghai,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Wen","family":"Zheng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Yantai University,School of Physics and Electronic Information,Yantai,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Xiaohu","family":"Yuan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Qiyuan Lab,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/iciibms66230.2025.11316762","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,2]]},"container-title":["2025 10th International Conference on Intelligent Informatics and Biomedical Sciences (ICIIBMS)"]},{"DOI":"10.1038\/s41598-026-50952-x","title":["A multi-agent large language model framework for intelligent vendor evaluation and risk-aware procurement decisions"],"author":[{"given":"Amey","family":"Joshi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mangal","family":"Singh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Deepak","family":"Parashar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mahesh","family":"Singh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1038\/s41598-026-50952-x","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,28]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>The process of vendor selection is a multi-dimensional and intricate task that requires analyzing the financial health, amount of risk, industry standards, and the mood in the marketplace. The use of traditional methods that consider individual data points or fixed metrics usually leads to less than optimal procurement decision-making. The current paper introduces a new Multi-Agent Large Language Model (LLM) framework that will improve the process of vendor evaluation based on the analysis of both structured and unstructured data. Its framework uses specialized agents that target different aspects of vendor evaluation: financial analysis, risk profiling, sentiment monitoring, and industry benchmarking. Structured indicators that financial agents consider include liquidity, profitability and solvency. Exposure to geopolitical, operational, and compliance risks is assessed by risk agents. Sentiment agents identify sentiment based on the news articles, reviews, and social media to determine the perception of the people and stakeholders. Agents in benchmarking compare vendors to industry standards to determine outliers and best-in-class vendors. The combination of these insights provides a data-driven yet context-aware procurement recommendation generated by the framework. The qualitative assessments are used to complement quantitative metrics, allowing procurement teams to make informed and holistic decisions. The system does not only help in identifying the most appropriate vendors but also assists in managing relationships with suppliers in the long term whereby the system alerts of any risk and performance deviations in real time. The method enables organizations to concentrate vendor selection in tandem with strategic objectives, reduce risk, and enhance the long-term value. The suggested multi-Agent LLM scheme is a major development in smart procurement systems, which enhance more resilient and value-oriented supply chains.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Scientific Reports"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/bigdata66926.2025.11401542","title":["A Large Language Model-Enabled Framework for Simulating Multi-Agent Cooperative Game"],"author":[{"given":"Xinrui","family":"Tao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Artificial Intelligence, Chongqing Technology and Business University,Chongqing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Qiaoling","family":"Shen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Artificial Intelligence, Chongqing Technology and Business University,Chongqing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Qiushuang","family":"Pu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Artificial Intelligence, Chongqing Technology and Business University,Chongqing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Quyuan","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Artificial Intelligence, Chongqing Technology and Business University,Chongqing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Fan","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Pengcheng Laboratory,Shenzhen,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/bigdata66926.2025.11401542","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,8]]},"container-title":["2025 IEEE International Conference on Big Data (BigData)"]},{"DOI":"10.5117\/ccr2026.2.8.li","title":["Large Language Model-driven Multi-Agent Simulation for Fake News Diffusion Under Different Network Structures"],"author":[{"family":"Xinyi Li","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Industrial Engineering and Management Science, Northwestern University, Illinois, US"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"family":"Yu Xu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Integrated Marketing Communications, Northwestern University, Illinois, US"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"family":"Yongfeng Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Computer Science, Rutgers University, New Jersey, US"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"family":"Edward Malthouse","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Integrated Marketing Communications, Northwestern University, Illinois, US"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5117\/ccr2026.2.8.li","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,31]]},"container-title":["Computational Communication Research"]},{"DOI":"10.31224\/6085","title":["Learning Evolving Latent Strategies for Multi-Agent Language Systems without Model Fine-Tuning"],"author":[{"given":"Wenlong","family":"Tang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.31224\/6085","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,24]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>This study proposes a multi-agent language framework that enables continual strategy evolution without fine-tuning the language model\u2019s parameters. The core idea is to liberate the latent vectors of abstract concepts from traditional \u201cstatic semantic representations,\u201d allowing them to be continuously updated through environmental interaction and reinforcement feedback. We construct a dual-loop architecture: the behavior loop adjusts action preferences based on environmental rewards, while the language loop updates the external latent vectors by reflecting on the semantic embeddings of generated text.\nTogether, these mechanisms allow agents to develop stable and disentangled strategic styles over long-horizon multi-round interactions. Experiments show that agents\u2019 latent spaces exhibit clear convergence trajectories under reflection-driven updates, along with structured shifts at critical moments. Moreover, the system demonstrates an emergent ability to implicitly infer and continually adopt emotional agents, even without shared rewards. These results indicate that, without modifying model parameters, an external latent space can provide language agents with a low-cost, scalable, and interpretable form of abstract strategic representation<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-9254050\/v1","title":["Automated Extraction of Patient Demographics from Clinical Vignettes Using a Multi-Agent Large Language Model Framework"],"author":[{"given":"Sudeshna","family":"Das","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Emory University"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Rand","family":"Ibrahim","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Emory University"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Nkele","family":"Davis","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Emory University"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yao","family":"Ge","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"National Institutes of Health"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yuting","family":"Guo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Emory University"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Abeed","family":"Sarker","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Emory University"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Marta","family":"Rowh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Emory University"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-9254050\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,8]]},"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>\n                  Background.\n Clinical vignettes are widely used in medical education to teach clinical reasoning, evaluate diagnostic skills, and contextualize patient care. These vignettes often include demographic information such as age, sex, race, ethnicity, and other identity markers that may influence learners\u2019 understanding of patient diversity. Inclusion of underrepresented populations is particularly important when demographic factors may affect diagnosis and care. Prior research shows that demographic details in clinical educational materials may be presented inconsistently or selectively, reflecting elements of hidden curriculum and reinforcing stereotypes. However, manually extracting demographic information from clinical vignettes in curricular materials for audits is resource-intensive, while existing automated approaches show limited reliability.\nMethods.\n We propose a multi-agentic framework to automatically extract demographic information from clinical vignettes. Our framework consists of three stages: (1) a few-shot learning step to identify clinical vignettes, (2) a few-shot infused demographic information extraction step in which multiple parallel agents extract demographic data from identified vignettes; and (3) an LLM-as-a-judge validation step in which extracted information is autonomously reviewed to correct potential errors. We employed this framework on a corpus of 156 instructional materials containing 264 clinical vignettes across 11 medical specialties from two US medical schools. A dataset of 56 clinical vignettes from 25 curricular material units was annotated by two clinicians across 16 demographic categories for in-context learning and model evaluation using F\n                  <sub>1<\/sub>\n                  score.\nResults.\n Inter-annotator agreement between clinicians was high (average Krippendorff's alpha: 0.83). Clinical vignette identification achieved F\n                  <sub>1<\/sub>\n                  score of 1.0 and demographic information extraction across all categories achieved average F\n                  <sub>1<\/sub>\n                  score of 0.87. Few-shot extraction with autonomous error correction improved performance by 8% compared to zero-shot demographic extraction. The validation stage corrected 27.78% of errors from the initial extraction.\nConclusion.\n A multi-agent LLM framework can effectively automate the identification of vignettes and extraction of demographic information from medical curricular materials. Multi-agent few-shot detection and autonomous correction significantly improves over zero-shot or single-agent methods. The ability to accurately audit large-scale educational corpora provides a scalable solution for medical educators to identify and address demographic representation gaps.\n                <\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.47472\/hhz6gcfu","title":["A Large Language Model-driven Multi-agent Framework For Inclusive Land Use Planning In Urban Renewal"],"author":[{"given":"Xiaoxin","family":"Yang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Wei","family":"Gan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Huixin","family":"Fu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiang","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.47472\/hhz6gcfu","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 61st ISOCARP World Planning Congress"]},{"DOI":"10.1039\/d4dd00013g","title":["ProtAgents: protein discovery\n                    <i>via<\/i>\n                    large language model multi-agent collaborations combining physics and machine learning"],"author":[{"given":"Alireza","family":"Ghafarollahi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Laboratory for Atomistic and Molecular Mechanics (LAMM), Massachusetts Institute of Technology, 77 Massachusetts Ave., Cambridge, MA 02139, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4173-9659","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Markus J.","family":"Buehler","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Laboratory for Atomistic and Molecular Mechanics (LAMM), Massachusetts Institute of Technology, 77 Massachusetts Ave., Cambridge, MA 02139, USA"},{"name":"Center for Computational Science and Engineering, Schwarzman College of Computing, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, 77 Massachusetts Ave., Cambridge, MA 02139, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1039\/d4dd00013g","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>\n                    ProtAgents is a\n                    <jats:italic>de novo<\/jats:italic>\n                    protein design platform based on multimodal LLMs, where distinct AI agents with expertise in knowledge retrieval, protein structure analysis, physics-based simulations, and results analysis tackle tasks in a dynamic setting.\n                  <\/jats:p>","container-title":["Digital Discovery"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/isctis70043.2026.11572301","title":["A Large Language Model-Enabled Multi-Agent Collaboration Method for Complex Task Solving"],"author":[{"given":"Shuyuan","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Brandeis University,Waltham,MA,USA,02453"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yihui","family":"Feng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Rose-Hulman Institute of Technology,Terre Haute,IN,USA,47803"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiaotian","family":"Fang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Cornell University,Ithaca,NY,USA,14850"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/isctis70043.2026.11572301","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,15]]},"container-title":["2026 6th International Symposium on Computer Technology and Information Science (ISCTIS)"]},{"DOI":"10.3390\/electronics15163694","title":["A Formal Trustworthiness Construct for Large Language Model-Based Test Generation: A Multidimensional Index Empirically Evaluated Through a Multi-Agent Study"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9252-0326","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Asta","family":"Slotkien\u0117","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Fundamental Sciences, Vilnius Gediminas Technical University, LT-10223 Vilnius, Lithuania"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lukas","family":"Makaris","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Fundamental Sciences, Vilnius Gediminas Technical University, LT-10223 Vilnius, Lithuania"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/electronics15163694","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,18]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Software code testing remains a critically important but labour-intensive process in software quality assurance. Existing research evaluates large language model (LLM)-based unit test generation using various quality metrics, such as correctness, coverage, mutation score, and test code smells. However, these single metrics do not reflect the trustworthiness of the unit test generation process. Therefore, this research formalises the trustworthiness of LLM-based unit test generation as a multidimensional index comprising reliability, hallucination resistance, maintainability, functional completeness, and human-reference alignment. In this research, we investigate the effect of prompt engineering strategies on the trustworthiness of LLM-generated unit tests and compare them with human-written tests for the same focal methods. Each dimension is fed by a distinct artefact-level measurement and grounded in dependability theory and ISO\/IEC 25010:2023. A centralised multi-agent system generates, builds, repairs, and measures the tests, so that all inputs are collected automatically. The index is evaluated on real-world C# focal methods across 18 model \u00d7 prompt configurations and a paired human-written baseline. The human baseline achieves the highest T-UTG value (0.904), and the best configuration, Combined \u00d7 Gemini, achieves 0.788. Entropy weighting identifies maintainability and hallucination resistance as the most discriminating dimensions, and a rank-acceptability analysis over the whole weight simplex confirms that this ordering does not depend on the chosen weighting scheme.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Electronics"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5551121","title":["Large language model-empowered dynamic scheduling for intelligent hybrid flow shop using multi-agent deep reinforcement learning"],"author":[{"given":"Wenbin","family":"Gu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yushang","family":"Cao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yuxin","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Nuandong","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Lei","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Na","family":"Tang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Minghai","family":"Yuan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5551121","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]}},{"DOI":"10.18653\/v1\/2026.findings-acl.7","title":["MALLM-GAN: Multi-Agent Large Language Model as Generative Adversarial Network for Synthesizing Tabular Data"],"author":[{"given":"Yaobin","family":"Ling","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiaoqian","family":"Jiang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yejin","family":"Kim","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.18653\/v1\/2026.findings-acl.7","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2026"]},{"DOI":"10.33774\/coe-2025-xkwl5","title":["Controlling Large Language Model Hallucination Based on Agent AI with LangGraph"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-3286-8690","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jialin","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Executive Vice President,Ferret Relationship Intelligence"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zhihua","family":"Duan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Intelligent Cloud Network Monitoring Department China Telecom Shanghai Company"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.33774\/coe-2025-xkwl5","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,13]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>As Large Language Models (LLMs) become integral to Natural Language Processing (NLP) applications, their tendency to generate hallucinations \u2014 outputs misaligned with input data or real-world facts \u2014 presents a significant challenge. This paper introduces a novel approach leveraging Agent AI within the LangGraph system to systematically detect and mitigate LLM hallucinations. The framework categorizes hallucinations into three distinct types: Hallucination Knowledge Positive (HK+), Hallucination Knowledge Negative (HK-), and normal responses. HK+ represents errors where the model has relevant knowledge but provides incorrect answers, while HK- denotes hallucinations arising from insufficient knowledge. Normal responses are accurate outputs requiring no intervention. The proposed method employs corrective Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) to address HK- cases by supplementing missing knowledge. For HK+, a human-in-the-loop mechanism is activated to rectify errors and ensure accuracy. Normal responses proceed without additional processing. This structured approach improves the precision and reliability of LLMs across diverse application scenarios. By integrating Agent AI for dynamic classification and targeted intervention, the LangGraph system significantly reduces hallucination rates, offering a robust solution for enhancing LLM performance in real-world deployments. This research provides new perspectives and methodologies for advancing LLM stability and dependability.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.173156111.18353230\/v1","title":["Large Language Model-Driven Immersive Agent"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0082-8364","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Aditi","family":"Singh","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Saket","family":"Kumar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Abul","family":"Ehtesham","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tala Talaei","family":"Khoei","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Deepshikha","family":"Bhati","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.173156111.18353230\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,14]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Recent research in the field of Large Language Models (LLMs) has given a new direction to the capabilities of AI agents for solving complex problems. This paper attempts to explore one such use case to investigate LLMs-based AI agents' role in immersive technology, specifically focusing on GPT-4's vision capabilities in Augmented Reality (AR). The paper utilizes Smart App Agent Framework for recommending products. This recommendation system assists users to make context-aware decisions during their online shopping experience.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/icnlp65360.2025.11108428","title":["Mitigating Bias in Large Language Models Leveraging Multi-Agent Scenarios"],"author":[{"given":"Jens","family":"L\u00fcnstedt","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"IU International University of Applied Sciences,Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tim","family":"Schlippe","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"IU International University of Applied Sciences,Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icnlp65360.2025.11108428","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,21]]},"container-title":["2025 7th International Conference on Natural Language Processing (ICNLP)"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-026-09751-x","title":["Chat with UAV \u2013 human-UAV interaction based on large language models"],"author":[{"given":"Haoran","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zhuohang","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Guang","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Bo","family":"Ma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chuanhuang","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-026-09751-x","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,4]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"]},{"DOI":"10.32604\/cmc.2026.077691","title":["Multi-Agent Large Language Model-Based Decision Tree Analysis for Explainable Electric Vehicle Drive Motor Fault Diagnosis"],"author":[{"given":"Jaeseung","family":"Lee","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jehyeok","family":"Rew","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.32604\/cmc.2026.077691","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Computers, Materials &amp; Continua"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6736926","title":["A knowledge graph-constrained multi-agent workflow for constructing a benchmark for large language model evaluation in traditional Chinese medicine"],"author":[{"given":"Shuqi","family":"Xia","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jingyao","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jinghua","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Wei","family":"Jiang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yang","family":"Wu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Tong","family":"Yu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6736926","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Background and Objective: Generic benchmarks rarely capture specialized clinical reasoning that requires heterogeneous evidence and multistep inference. We developed a knowledge graph-constrained multi-agent workflow, named MAC-TCM, for constructing benchmarks in traditional Chinese medicine, and instantiated it as ZBN-Bench for the Zhao Bingnan academic school of dermatology.Methods: A domain knowledge base was assembled from over 1000 curated clinical cases, 54 books, over 1000 full-text articles, and over 3000 indexed records, and encoded as a graph of 3300 nodes and 12,370 edges along the symptom-syndrome-disease-treatment-formula-herb chain. Candidate items were produced under graph-path and question-direction constraints, filtered by answer-reachability validation and four-dimensional soft scoring. An external sample of 300 items was independently rated by four domain experts, and fourteen large language models were evaluated under a prespecified zero-shot protocol.Results: Entity extraction against manual review reached 92.4% precision and 89.6% sensitivity (Cohen\u2019s kappa 0.85). The automatic quality filter showed high specificity against low-quality candidates, reducing factual errors from 27.2% under unconstrained generation to 5.4%. The final benchmark contains 1500 items (750 single-choice, 450 multiple-choice, 300 chained-choice), with an expert consensus score of 3.802 out of 4.0 and inter-rater Fleiss\u2019 kappa 0.78. Automatic scoring agreed closely with expert consensus (mean absolute error 0.035). The best-performing model reached 62.30% overall accuracy, with degradation on high-difficulty (26.54%) and chained-choice (18.62%) items.Conclusions: The workflow provides a traceable software pipeline and a reproducibility-oriented protocol for constructing specialty benchmarks in traditional Chinese medicine. ZBN-Bench offers a school-specific evaluation resource for model benchmarking and methodological research, although broader external validation remains necessary before downstream clinical interpretation.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.envres.2025.121401","title":["Multi-agent large language model frameworks: Unlocking new possibilities for optimizing wastewater treatment operation"],"author":[{"given":"Samuel","family":"Rothfarb","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mikayla","family":"Friday","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xingyu","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Arash","family":"Zaghi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0401-578X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Baikun","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.envres.2025.121401","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,6]]},"container-title":["Environmental Research"]},{"DOI":"10.1038\/s41598-026-42705-7","title":["When collaboration fails: persuasion driven adversarial influence in multi agent large language model debate"],"author":[{"given":"Insaf","family":"Kraidia","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Iyas","family":"Qaddara","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Alhanof","family":"Almutairi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Nada","family":"Alzaben","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Samir Brahim","family":"Belhouari","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1038\/s41598-026-42705-7","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,8]]},"container-title":["Scientific Reports"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.eml.2024.102131","title":["MechAgents: Large language model multi-agent collaborations can solve mechanics problems, generate new data, and integrate knowledge"],"author":[{"given":"Bo","family":"Ni","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Markus J.","family":"Buehler","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.eml.2024.102131","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,3]]},"container-title":["Extreme Mechanics Letters"]},{"DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-9262455\/v1","title":["Neurosymbolic Multi-Agent Large Language Model System Versus Specialist Physicians in COPD and Asthma Management: A Comparative Performance Evaluation Using Guideline-Based Clinical Vignettes"],"author":[{"given":"Ebru Aykan","family":"Mavigoz","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Etimesgut Asker Hastanesi"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Mete","family":"Ucdal","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Etimesgut Asker Hastanesi"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Evren","family":"Ekingen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Etimesgut Asker Hastanesi"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-9262455\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,27]]},"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>\n                  <bold>Background<\/bold>\n                  Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) and asthma collectively affect over 500 million individuals worldwide and represent leading causes of respiratory-related morbidity, healthcare utilization, and preventable mortality. Optimal management of these conditions encompasses accurate diagnosis and differential diagnosis, severity stratification, stepwise pharmacological therapy, exacerbation management, ventilatory support decisions, and complication recognition, all of which require the integration of subspecialty expertise that is frequently fragmented across clinical settings. Neurosymbolic multi-agent large language model (LLM) systems, which integrate neural reasoning with formal symbolic logic and specialized agent architectures, represent a promising paradigm for bridging domain-specific expertise gaps across pulmonology, allergology, and emergency medicine.\n                  <bold>Methods<\/bold>\n                  We conducted a cross-sectional comparative evaluation using 30 USMLE-format clinical vignettes spanning six COPD and asthma management domains, namely diagnosis and differential diagnosis, severity assessment, pharmacological management, exacerbation management, ICU admission and ventilatory management, and complication management, all derived from the current GOLD 2025COPD and GINA 2025 Asthma guidelines. A neurosymbolic multi-agent system (NS-MAS) comprising a LangGraph orchestrator coordinating eight specialized agents, including GPT-4.5, Claude Sonnet 4.6, and Gemini 2.5 Pro, was compared against 30 board-certified specialist physicians consisting of 10 pulmonologists, 10 allergists and clinical immunologists, and 10 emergency medicine physicians. The primary outcome was overall accuracy. Statistical analyses included the Kruskal-Wallis test, Dunn's post-hoc test with Bonferroni correction, and effect size estimation using Cohen's d.\n                  <bold>Results<\/bold>\n                  NS-MAS achieved an overall accuracy of 90.0% (27\/30), significantly exceeding all physician groups: pulmonologists 75.0% (mean 22.5 \u00b1 2.1\/30, p = 0.002, d = 1.82), allergists\/immunologists 76.0% (mean 22.8 \u00b1 2.2\/30, p = 0.003, d = 1.74), and emergency physicians 62.0% (mean 18.6 \u00b1 2.0\/30, p &lt; 0.001, d = 2.84). Domain-specific analysis revealed significant specialty-expertise interactions: allergists outperformed pulmonologists in exacerbation management (84% vs 72%, p = 0.003) and pharmacological management (82% vs 76%, p = 0.018), while pulmonologists demonstrated superior performance in ICU\/ventilatory management (66% vs 58%, p &lt; 0.001) and complication management domains (66% vs 62%, p = 0.028). NS-MAS response latency (median 13.1 seconds) was significantly shorter than all physician groups (median 2.9\u20133.5 minutes, p &lt; 0.001). Inter-rater reliability among physicians was fair to moderate (\u03ba = 0.36\u20130.49).\n                  <bold>Conclusions<\/bold>\n                  A neurosymbolic multi-agent LLM system demonstrated comprehensive, guideline-concordant COPD and asthma management performance surpassing all specialist physician groups across nearly all clinical domains. The system's ability to integrate domain-specific reasoning across pulmonology, allergology, and emergency medicine knowledge bases, guided simultaneously by the GOLD 2025and GINA 2025 frameworks, suggests meaningful clinical decision support potential, particularly in resource-limited or cross-specialty settings. These findings support further prospective clinical validation.\n                <\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.70456\/igfo3364","title":["APPLICATION OF LARGE LANGUAGE MODELS AND NATURAL LANGUAGE UNDERSTANDING IN MULTI-AGENT TOURIST GUIDE FOR GABROVO"],"author":[{"given":"Iliya","family":"Nedelchev","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.70456\/igfo3364","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>This paper explores the integration of Large Language Models (LLM) and Natural Language Understanding (NLU) within a multi-agent tourist guide for Gabrovo. In the context of the digital transformation of the tourism industry, this study examines how advanced NLP technologies enhance the tourist experience. The multi-agent tourist guide, powered by LLM and NLU, delivers personalized recommendations, historical insights, and real-time assistance to travelers. This research emphasizes the transformative potential of these technologies in tourism by fostering engagement, promoting cultural immersion, and streamlining navigation. Furthermore, the study highlights the broader implications of AI-driven language models and understanding in preserving cultural heritage. By harnessing LLM and NLU, this tourist guide redefines exploration in Gabrovo and symbolizes a shift in how individuals connect with cultural legacies worldwide. In essence, this paper underscores the significant impact of technology in reshaping multi-agent tourist guides, enriching traveler experiences, and preserving cultural identities in destinations such as Gabrovo.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["UNITECH \u2013 SELECTED PAPERS"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/ijcnn64981.2025.11227551","title":["Neural Algorithmic Reasoners informed Large Language Model for Multi-Agent Path Finding"],"author":[{"given":"Pu","family":"Feng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Beihang University,State Key Laboratory of Complex &#x0026; Critical Software Environment,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Size","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beihang University,School of Artificial Intelligence,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yuhong","family":"Cao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"National University of Singapore,Department of Mechanical Engineering,Singapore"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Junkang","family":"Liang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beihang University,School of Artificial Intelligence,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Rongye","family":"Shi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beihang University,State Key Laboratory of Complex &#x0026; Critical Software Environment,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Wenjun","family":"Wu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beihang University,State Key Laboratory of Complex &#x0026; Critical Software Environment,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/ijcnn64981.2025.11227551","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,6,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,6,30]]},"container-title":["2025 International Joint Conference on Neural Networks (IJCNN)"]},{"DOI":"10.21125\/iceri.2025.1903","title":["REIMAGINING AI AGENTS FOR EDUCATION: THE EVOLUTION TOWARDS COLLABORATIVE LARGE LANGUAGE MODEL MULTI-AGENT SYSTEMS"],"author":[{"given":"Fabrizio","family":"Lo Presti","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6288-0830","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Manuel","family":"Gentile","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Giuseppina Rita Jose","family":"Mangione","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-8399-2871","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Lydia","family":"Zampolini","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21125\/iceri.2025.1903","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11]]},"container-title":["ICERI Proceedings","ICERI2025 Proceedings"]}],"items-per-page":100,"query":{"start-index":0,"search-terms":null}}}